Only these route-local modules plus report-level claims explicitly marked canonical in `docs/state/theorem-state.yaml` are canonical Target C theorem sources at audit.91. Sections 110--121 are canonical report-level entries and are linked below. The exact train-coupled floor endpoint exclusion is also canonical; its detailed proof and independent audit are linked below. The file `95-protected-contact-creation-obstruction-audit.md` remains proof-only and is intentionally outside the canonical reading order.

## Scope rule

The §57 ZR/ZF theorem is on the nonisolated positive-contact core `V`, not the full source:

\[
\ker\Omega_V=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1,P_x|_V,P_y|_V\},
\qquad
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}[V]=2v-3.
\]

Contact-isolated source points remain in the global configuration. Do not extend the affine-kernel conclusion to their coordinates.

## Current route

Audit.127 independently promotes the [overlap-aware owner root/run capacity](../../audits/target_c_overlap_aware_owner_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md). Its pairwise-disjoint acute-binary cut-bank hypothesis closes the owner-to-train allocation gap from Audit.63; it supplies no full-source move and leaves the C-II route unchanged.

Audit.128 records the exact three-edge long-pocket anchor regression. It prevents extending the short-pocket terminal determinant bound using only local convexity and unit separation; it has no actual-load or global-minimizer conclusion and does not change the route.

Audit.129 promotes `C-ATOMIC-TRACK-BOUNDARY-COST`, an all-source Type-II boundary budget. It forces a linear perimeter gap when `t_D=(q-k)/D<=1.860+o(1)`, so the joint low-`t_D`, `x_B=O(sqrt(D))` subcase is empty. Theorem A alone gives `q/D<=2.6829854059+o(1)`; the verifier's stronger constant check is for Theorem B, not A. See the [independent Audit.129](../../audits/target_c_atomic_track_boundary_cost_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.130 promotes `C-ATOMIC-OUTER-ROUTE-EXPOSURE`. The full occurrence-counted outer walk exposes at least one direction outside any finite track-atom neighborhoods on all but its excess number x_B of loaded routes. Consequently `liminf (Q+4f+x_B)/D>=4s_2` and `liminf (10(q-k)+g-4z)/D>=5pi+4s_2`. At low t_D, this gives `g-4z >= (0.9172144954-o(1))D` and `q/D<=2.2243781581850+o(1)`. The verifier's constant check matches this endpoint but does not verify the analytic theorem. No C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.130](../../audits/target_c_atomic_outer_route_exposure_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.131 promotes `C-ATOMIC-ONE-END-TRAIN-BOUNDARY`: counting every rhombus track and every singleton contact edge gives `liminf (2f-k+m)/D>=2s_2`, hence `liminf (5(q-k)+2g-z-u_B)/D>=3pi+2s_2`. Thus `x_B=o(D)` is impossible below `(q-k)/D=2.2658807149-o(1)`. Combined with Audit.130, the low-`t_D` branch has `g/D>=1.0147017872-o(1)` and `q/D<=2.1268908664+o(1)`, still compatible with the canonical lower bound on `q`. C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.131](../../audits/target_c_atomic_one_end_train_boundary_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.132 promotes the sharp orbit detour theorem `lambda>=Phi(A)` for any direction measure whose projection widths are at least two, together with the necessary Type-II limit constraint `4s_2<=sqrt((pi+lambda)Phi^{-1}(lambda))+5(2t-pi-lambda)`. It bounds each three-direction orbit but does not force a positive endpoint-orbit mass. The missing source bridge remains open; see the [independent Audit.132](../../audits/target_c_atomic_orbit_sharp_detour_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.133 promotes the finite unloaded-rhombus separator capacity and its Menger consequence: `p>=ceil(F(R))` vertex-disjoint actual contact paths to distinct loaded attachments, where `F(R)=(sqrt(25+(32pi/sqrt(3))R)-5)/(16pi/sqrt(3))`. An explicitly convex unit-contact face of degree `m` with `ell` loaded boundary vertices forces at least `ceil((m-2ell)_+/(4pi+2))` such paths. Under explicit but unpromoted host-cap extraction hypotheses, quadratic grain area also gives linear long returns and quadratic outside population; that result is conditional. A pinned-grid regression limits what follows from pin-only equilibrium and rank. The route paths are structural and are not finite motions or exchange currency, so the remaining C-II task is to pay them against train/interface/face budgets or use their endpoint placement in a full-source strict-diameter replacement. See the [independent Audit.133](../../audits/target_c_rhombus_bulk_separator_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.134 promotes `C-FIXED-PORT-CONTACT-ROUTE-BOUND` and `C-BOUNDED-FACE-PORT-ROUTE-BUDGET`. A specified loaded attachment reaches any unloaded vertex in its component by an actual contact path of length at most `C R+C_0+10(e_BB-k)`, where `C=4/sqrt(3)+30pi` and `C_0=90pi+30`; at fixed face-degree cutoff, a route that is long compared with its Euclidean displacement forces a linear high-degree face tail. The global tail is bounded once by the canonical Section-123 currency, without a route-multiplicity conclusion. The rational rhombus regression refutes factor two for arbitrary boundary detours, while the promoted `4/sqrt(3)` estimate holds. See the [independent Audit.134](../../audits/target_c_fixed_port_route_bound_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.175 records the cap-local positive-height grid implication as conditional on an explicit same-cell macroscopic cap package. Audit.173's canonical local-grid theorem removes that premise for grid existence near every Type-II attachment. Combining it with the canonical fixed-port route budget gives a conditional global-tail alternative at every attachment: uniform tail vanishing yields short access to every grid vertex, while a persistent fixed-radius obstruction forces a linear tail at every fixed degree cutoff. The cap package and tail condition remain unproved; no route-multiplicity bound or source move follows. The C-II finite-move/payment obligation is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.175](../../audits/target_c_near_port_conditional_promotion_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.176 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-SHALLOW-WALL-RELEASE-CAPACITY`: for one actual hull-augmented cell with total unit-path turn `T<=2*pi/3`, every weak packet guarded at all path vertices can be translated and slightly dilated into the strict region. The weak packet capacity is exactly `|S_Q|`, so there is no fixed-cell weak-contact surplus in this scope. It does not extract such a cell from a canonical source or handle omitted guards, moving walls, or multiple cells. The exact larger-turn regression `C-LARGER-TURN-COLLECTIVE-TRANSLATION-REGRESSION` refutes inferring a common packet translation from per-site non-surrounding; its source is nonglobal and does admit a finite nonlinear release. C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.176](../../audits/target_c_shallow_wall_packet_release_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.177 promotes `C-FULL-SOURCE-ISOLATE-NORMALIZATION` and `C-CORE-DIAMETER-REPLACEMENT-AFTER-ISOLATE-NORMALIZATION`. From any already feasible same-cardinality exact-contact full-source endpoint with diameter at most `D`, a centroid contraction strictly opens every isolate-involving diameter equality while retaining all labels and contacts; the canonical noncongruent-core stress theorem and full-source diameter-pair minimum then force at least `L` replacement diameter pairs inside the core for `L` shortened old pairs. This sharpens endpoint accounting but constructs no endpoint and leaves C-II open. See the [independent Audit.177](../../audits/target_c_isolate_normalization_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.178 promotes `C-ACTUAL-DIAMETER-CUBIC-MOMENT` and `C-CORE-DIAMETER-REPLACEMENT-COVER-COST`. The finite-source limit of the canonical near-diameter moment bounds the actual diameter degree excess at every noncollinear global minimizer. At an already feasible normalized endpoint, any changed-label cover of the new core diameter bank has cost `2s+C_0^(1/3)s^(2/3)D^(2/3)`, and its maximum matching has the corresponding `4nu+2^(2/3)C_0^(1/3)nu^(2/3)D^(2/3)` bound. Linear lost-pair demand requires support of order at least `sqrt(D)`, but no endpoint is constructed and C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.178](../../audits/target_c_actual_diameter_cubic_replacement_cover_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.137 records `C-DEEP-FAR-COMPLETE-GRID-LOADED-RETURNS` conditionally. With an explicit coherent quadratic grain and convex cap package, the deep/far trimming leaves a quadratic component with a complete linear-by-linear grid and linearly many vertex-disjoint returns to distinct actual loaded endpoints. The returns contain quadratically many distinct internal vertices outside the grid. The cap/grain extraction and the payment step remain open, so the C-II finite-move/payment obligation does not change. See the [independent Audit.137](../../audits/target_c_deep_far_cap_grid_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.138 promotes `C-DIAMETER-SUPPORT-CROSSCUT-HUB`: for a finite proper pairwise-disjoint crosscut system, pairwise alternating or endpoint-sharing boundary diameter pairs have support subtrees with a common dual-tree region. Endpoint marks on cuts are handled using all incident regions. The common-hub theorem alone does not show that a nonhub branch lacks a complete diameter pair; an exact endpoint-on-cut regression records this limit. Audit.148 independently audits the short-pair tube estimate, replacing fourteen rows by a six-row Oler bound under its explicit path/crossing hypotheses. Audit.149 promotes the sharp five-point capsule packing `C-UNIT-CAPSULE-PACKING-5` and its supplied-path consequence `C-OO-SHORT-PAIR-PATH-BANDWIDTH-5` for segments of length at most `21/20`. Neither result extracts the path or supplies a motion, so there is no C-II route change. See the [independent Audit.138](../../audits/target_c_diameter_support_crosscut_hub_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md), [Audit.148](../../audits/target_c_oo_short_pair_path_bandwidth_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md), and [Audit.149](../../audits/target_c_five_row_capsule_packing_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.153 promotes `C-ROOTED-CROSSCUT-MARKED-INTERVAL-CAPACITY`: for any root of a dual tree from pairwise disjoint proper crosscuts, marks assigned to a component after root deletion form a consecutive boundary interval, so at most two consecutive marked-cycle pairs cross it. In the canonical O--O setting, loaded-loaded pairs of length at most `13/12` are hull sides; the two-per-branch clause count applies when each mark has the fixed single-region assignment used by the motion model. The broader claim that every nonhub branch is free of a complete diameter pair fails at crosscut-endpoint marks, as shown by Audit.138. The C-II obligation is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.153](../../audits/target_c_rooted_crosscut_branch_interval_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.154 promotes `C-OO-SINGLETON-BAD-TERMINAL-CAPACITY`: no singleton O--O bridge train can be fully good-ended, so every selected singleton consumes a distinct occurrence from the exact bad bank `x_B+m_B`. The proposed `14+x_B+m_B` dual-tree range still requires the fourteen-cut estimate that Audit.138 withheld for lack of an exact inequality and cut-to-rhombus proof. The singleton count alone supplies no bound on the remaining non-singleton path length. See the [independent Audit.154](../../audits/target_c_singleton_oo_clause_range_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.155 refines the finite contact-budget endpoint by splitting its cases: strict contact loss uses contact minimality and allows new pairs at distance `D`; equal contact count with removed old diameter pairs still requires pair-minimality and strict upper slack. The one-spare-site packet exclusion is a direct corollary, not a source-capacity theorem. See the [independent Audit.155](../../audits/target_c_one_spare_site_scope_refinement_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.157 records `C-TYPEII-CAP-LOCAL-HARMONIC-GRID` as audited-working, conditional on a fixed-area convex hull of actual loaded diameter endpoints. Under the canonical Type-II inputs, it yields a quadratic coherent rhombus grain and linear-by-linear phase-pure grid inside one unloaded harmonic cell. The source-level cap extraction and any finite move or payment remain open, so the C-II frontier is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.157](../../audits/target_c_cap_local_harmonic_grid_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.158 records `C-TYPEII-CAP-DEEP-SAME-HOST-ATTACHMENT` as audited-working when the quadratic-area endpoint cap lies on a proper loaded-boundary interval and contains the cap-local grain. It forces a loaded attachment on that interval at linear height and gives a linear arc/chord detour; it supplies the actual-attachment input of Audit.136's near-port grid theorem under the same cap package. No contact-path length, payment, source move, or C-II closure follows; cap and interval extraction remain open. See the [independent Audit.158](../../audits/target_c_deep_same_host_attachment_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.139 promotes the actual exterior-cap diameter separator and its finite reduced-source motions. A fixed-area cap forces all actual diameter edges across a line, placing the full diameter graph in the Woodall forest branch and keeping `q/D` a fixed amount below `pi`. The core cut removes at most `(2/sqrt(3)+1)D+2` unloaded labels and strictly reduces diameter while fixing every contact isolate; the full-source strip motion has a uniform permitted translation and deletes at most `(2a/(sqrt(3)/2)+1)D+2a+1` labels. Neither result supplies the cardinality-restoring packet. No C-II frontier change follows. See the [independent Audit.139](../../audits/target_c_exterior_cap_separator_motion_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.140 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-INTERNAL-WALL-ONE-GUARD-CAPACITY`: for one hull-augmented cell, omitting the guard at a single internal actual unit-wall vertex does not raise the strict packet capacity above `|S_Q|`. This rules out the report's proposed one-extra-site fixed-cell closure in a canonical source, while leaving moved-core, cross-cell, and weak-contact refills open. C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.140](../../audits/target_c_exterior_internal_wall_vertex_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.160 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-OCCUPIED-CAP-INTERNAL-WALL-VERTEX`: every occupied actual exterior cap's canonical hull-augmented cell has an internal actual unit-contact vertex on its boundary path. The cap's base endpoints are distinct and have separation greater than one, so their contact path cannot consist of a single edge. With Audit.140, the one-extra-site strict packet route is therefore closed in each occupied cap cell. This does not supply a packet, a moved-wall refill, or cross-cell compatibility; C-II remains open. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_occupied_cap_internal_wall_vertex_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.161 promotes `C-UNIT-WALL-DELAUNAY-DEFECT-BUDGET` and `C-UNIT-WALL-ONE-DEFICIT-DICHOTOMY` for arbitrary convex unit-edge walls. After maximal auxiliary completion, the Delaunay excess gives at most `4 sigma/delta` right/obtuse faces and `(450+12/delta) sigma` exceptional vertices. In the one-deficit range, the no-addition case has at most 51 right/obtuse faces, 137 defective faces, and 541 exceptional vertices; the one-addition case has the canonical low-slack chart. This does not localize total metric strain, give an exact vacancy in the original cloud, or supply a source move. C-II remains open. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_one_deficit_delaunay_defect_budget_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.162 promotes `C-FIVE-POINT-HULL-PERIMETER-LOWER-BOUND` and `C-UNIT-PENTAGON-PERIMETER-CAPACITY`. Any five mutually unit-separated points have convex-hull perimeter at least five; consequently the only five-point unit-separated subset of a strictly convex unit-sided pentagon is its vertex set. Deleting d>=1 vertices permits at most d-1 globally separated strict-interior replacement points clear of the retained vertices, and retaining all five permits none. A vertex-disjoint bank obeys the summed deficit under disjoint label assignment. This is a fixed-container count, not a full-source packet or diameter-improvement theorem; C-II remains open. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_unit_pentagon_perimeter_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.163 promotes `C-CONTACT-MINIMAL-HULL-COVERAGE`, `C-CONVEX-POLYGON-ANGULAR-AREA-BOUND`, `C-UNIT-VORONOI-CELL-AREA-SURPLUS`, `C-CANONICAL-NEAR-ISOLATE-POPULATION-BOUND`, and `C-FULL-SOURCE-SPACING-DEBIT`. For canonical noncollinear contact-minimal sources, the exact full-source Voronoi ledger bounds only the delta-near-isolated labels by (4*sqrt(3)/delta+8)D+1. The proof uses the scaled unit-packing Voronoi-cell area theorem, a new convex-polygon area bound, contact-minimal hull coverage, and the canonical strict population excess. The separately promoted spacing debit is a necessary Oler constraint on any partition with a more widely spaced Y subset. These results do not bound all contact isolates or close the C-II finite-move/payment obligation. See the [independent Audit.163](../../audits/target_c_full_source_voronoi_spacing_debit_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.164 promotes `C-FULL-SOURCE-DELAUNAY-DEFECT-BUDGET`, `C-FULL-SOURCE-DELAUNAY-NEAR-NEIGHBOR-BUDGET`, `C-FULL-SOURCE-TWO-LOWER-BLOCKERS`, and `C-LOWER-CLOSED-SET-SUPPORT-FRONT`, with `C-HULL-COVERED-THIN-BOUNDARY-TRIANGLE-REGRESSION`. The exact ledger retains all source labels and separates the boundary-face area from nonnegative bulk strain, yielding `Sigma<4*sqrt(3)D` and an `O(D+D/epsilon)` exception count for six near neighbors. Every bulk label has two individual strict blockers for any chosen short translation. Lower-closed sets put their backward support labels in that same exceptional set, but no root multiplicity, source motion, integer payment, or common phase follows. The thin regression guards against applying the bulk triangle bound to boundary faces. C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.164](../../audits/target_c_full_source_delaunay_strain_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).
Audit.165 promotes `C-GLOBAL-POPULATION-EXCESS-SQRT-RESERVE`. Every global unit-separated n-point diameter minimizer with `D>=3` satisfies `g_A=n-1-pi*D^2/(2*sqrt(3)) >= 3^(5/4)*sqrt(D)/(256*pi) > sqrt(D)/256`. This all-minimizer statement follows from a translated triangular-lattice disk count and two Fourier modes; it has no triangle-free or contact-core assumptions. It strengthens the population reserve in source-wide ledgers but supplies no source-preserving competitor, triangle conclusion, or C-II closure. See the [independent Audit.165](../../audits/target_c_lattice_population_reserve_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.166 promotes a finite full-source circumcircle safety and blocker-closure theorem, its canonical large-source consequence, the path-forest structure of actual degree-one two-contact fan leaves, and the associated KKT multiplier budgets. For every actual diameter endpoint in the canonical C-II source with `D>=10000`, the lower-blocker closure reaches support depth `D/2-o(D)` with asymptotic edge-gap excess at most `1/2`. For `U` qualifying fan leaves, their induced contact graph is a path forest and yields at least `ceil(U/2)` disjoint loaded-to-diameter-inactive first-blocker pairs; no lower bound on `U` follows. Neither result supplies disjoint paths, a shared payment, or a finite competitor, so C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.166](../../audits/target_c_half_depth_fan_forest_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.167 promotes `C-ODD-SHELL-CLEARANCE-FOLD-ENDPOINT` and `C-ODD-SHELL-COLLAR-TRANSPORT-ENDPOINT`, and records `C-ODD-SHELL-TWO-ISOLATE-FOLD-REGRESSION`. These are two source-preserving finite endpoints for regular odd shells: the wall fold applies for odd `q>=21` when every interior cloud point has clearance greater than `1+3/R` from every wall vertex; the collar-transport endpoint applies for odd `q>=65` with the prescribed Audit.65 collar and a deep cloud. Two fixed isolates block every cyclic wall-only fold when the cloud is held fixed, but that regression does not apply to the collar-transport move. The claims do not cover arbitrary canonical sources or change the C-II frontier. See the [independent Audit.167](../../audits/target_c_odd_shell_folds_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.168 records `C-CIRCUMCIRCLE-CENTERED-DISK-CAP-SHARPNESS-REGRESSION` and `C-FAN-PATH-COMMON-TRUNK-REGRESSION`. The first shows that the `1-sqrt(3)/2` leading coefficient is sharp for the centered radius-`D` disk cap. The second constructs, for every `q>=1`, a triangle-free nonglobal source with `q+1` disjoint two-contact diameter-fan triples and unique blocker paths sharing `floor(D/8)` contact-isolated labels; a triangular-lattice augmentation preserves the paths while attaining leading packing density and hull area `pi D^2/4-O(D)`. Neither regression has a normalized KKT stress or satisfies canonical global-source hypotheses. The centered cap and finite-closure lemmas in the source report are already subsumed by Audit.166, and the C-II frontier is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.168](../../audits/target_c_common_trunk_congestion_regression_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.169 promotes `C-NONCONTACT-WALL-WEAK-SHIELD`, `C-POST-DESCENT-WEAK-WALL-REFILL`, and `C-UNIT-SEPARATED-MOVING-WALL-CROSSING-BARRIER`. The weak shield allows exact guards on simple source-vertex walls with sides at most `sqrt(3)`; a strict reduced endpoint plus packets paying every deleted and old interior label gives a full-source sub-`D` competitor. A separate all-order nonglobal grid regression shows that a specified flat wall can retain capacity exactly equal to its old interior population after a strict reduced endpoint. This refutes spare capacity at that fixed wall from small phase error alone, with no canonical global-source or KKT conclusion. C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.169](../../audits/target_c_weak_wall_refill_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

- [Audit.169 weak-wall refill report](../../reports/target_c_post_descent_weak_wall_refill_capacity_plateau_2026-10-08.md)

Audit.141 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-CELL-WEAK-GUARD-LEDGER`: a weakly guarded packet in one hull-augmented cell preserves all full-source lower constraints, and its new unit contacts can involve only packet points or retained wall vertices. The complete contact and actual-diameter ledger then gives an exact exclusion criterion, including the strict sub-D case when all old diameters are deleted. Exact contacts are counted and the complete replacement must still be triangle-free for the lexicographic branch. This supplies no packet or moved-wall refill. C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.141](../../audits/target_c_exterior_cell_weak_guard_contact_ledger_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.142 records `C-RHOMBUS-EDGE-COMPLETION-COLLISION-REGRESSION`: a strict unit rhombus can contain the same-side equilateral completion of one side at distance below one from its opposite vertex. Completing a unit edge alone therefore does not supply a feasible interior packet site. The explicit four-point example is nonglobal and does not change the weak-guard ledger or C-II frontier. See the [independent Audit.142](../../audits/target_c_rhombus_edge_completion_collision_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.143 records `C-PARTIAL-WALL-INTERIOR-CHORD-ISOLATE-REGRESSION`: a nonunit side that looks like a partial-wall boundary can lie inside the actual source hull, leaving a retained isolate too close to an otherwise wall-safe site. This is a separate location-of-boundary guardrail; the exact example is not canonical or globally optimal, and it does not change C-II. See the [independent Audit.143](../../audits/target_c_partial_hull_interior_chord_isolate_regression_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.144 promotes `C-FINITE-SORTING-PACKET-RADIAL-LIFTING-STRESS` for the 33 listed finite cases: the exact radial-height lift yields a positive pinned stress on every unit contact row touching a moving site. The packets contain unit triangles; the result is neither an all-`M` stress theorem nor a global-source statement, and it does not change C-II. See the [independent Audit.144](../../audits/target_c_finite_radial_lifting_stress_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.145 promotes `C-STRICT-TURN-PATH-CONTACT-NON-SURROUNDING`: a site cannot be strictly surrounded by contacts to a convex unit path of total turn below pi when internal vertices are weakly guarded and no path edge has both endpoints unit-adjacent to it. Other source constraints still need strict margins before the canonical one-site motion applies. See the [independent Audit.145](../../audits/target_c_strict_turn_path_non_surrounding_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.150 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-CAP-LOCAL-STRESS-TRANSVERSALS`: each cut inside an actual exterior-cap separator has aggregate crossing-contact stress projection at least `2g` and an unloaded core stress transversal of size at most `s_Z+1`. Fixed-area caps give `Omega(D)` disjoint choices, but these yield separate reduced-source descents only; there is no simultaneous motion, refill, or C-II frontier change. See the [independent Audit.150](../../audits/target_c_exterior_cap_local_stress_transversal_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.152 promotes `C-CENTERED-FAN-BLOCKER-DEPTH-PATH`: each supplied canonical degree-two diameter-fan leaf has a near-unit lower-blocker path reaching support depth `D/10`, with at least `D/11` edges and bounded total gap excess. If no other nonisolated source point is reached first, the path contains at least `D/11-1` contact isolates. This per-leaf localization gives no path-disjointness, face-currency charge, or competitor; the C-II frontier is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.152](../../audits/target_c_centered_fan_blocker_closure_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.64 retains the complete audit.52--63 stack, including terminal O--O depletion, and adds exact finite contact-budget and low-contact packet-opening endpoints. Audit.155 separates the contact-loss branch, which needs only contact minimality, from the equal-contact diameter-pair branch.

Audit.65 adds the odd-shell blocker-payment theorem and an exact nonglobal-family regression. For regular odd `q>=65`, each strict interior contact isolate blocks at most one adjacent wall side, so fully blocking the inward one-vertex proposals requires at least `(q+1)/2` isolates. The constructed blocked quadratic filling is strictly beaten by a whole-source packet; this does not yield arbitrary-container capacity or change the C-II endpoint.

Audit.66 adds a collective exterior shield for a pairwise-distance-at-least-two set of deleted wall vertices, and a greedy `ceil(M/25)` bank from `1`-separated wall vertices. The finite replacement implication is subsumed by Audit.64. An exact D(8) heptagon-plus-center source shows that the wall-safe region can have capacity one even at a global minimizer; the capacity obstacle therefore needs a genuinely asymptotic canonical hypothesis.

Audit.67 proves a conditional isolate-preserving inward release in one explicit curved-wall chamber. In its canonical blocker-count form, each blocked root requires a distinct interior source point at distance below one; the blocker may be a contact-core point and need not be an isolate. The exact local regression and scope are in the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_curved_wall_independent_audit_2026-10-06.md). This does not change the C-II frontier.

Audit.68 uses Oler's finite bound to show that every source in a strict-area near-triangular curved-wall chamber has at most `(M-1)^2` interior points. Audit.67's explicit packet has exactly that capacity, so replacing the full interior and a separated wall bank excludes the chamber from canonical sources. The strict area condition has a nonempty parameter family for every fixed `M>=4`; extraction of such a chamber from an arbitrary canonical source remains open.

Audit.69 extends this exclusion to two independent uneven opposite-side angular profiles for `M>=8`. The discrete Green-function lower bound provides a positive clearance margin on the middle wall bank. A concentrated-curvature corner root fails exactly, so the bank restriction is part of the theorem. No arbitrary-source chamber extraction or C-II closure follows.

Audit.70 proves a strategy regression for the full three-direction triangular-lattice interior packet: retaining all six radial corner guards prevents every near-identity homothety and every C2 affine packet family through the identity. Remote affine packets, nonaffine rearrangements, and guard-deletion exchanges remain open.

Audit.71 supplies an independently audited local stress requirement for the nonaffine packet route. In the complete radius-`M-1` triangular-lattice chart with six guards whose second-order inward coefficient is `C=M(M^2-1)/6`, each nested ring `r=0,...,M-1` must satisfy `E_r >= 12C`. This is a necessary condition for a one-sided C2 feasible family; overlapping ring inequalities cannot be added as independent energy. The corresponding energy upper bound and any extraction from a canonical source remain open.

Audit.72 proves a complementary local packet result for a specified six-sided curved wall. Removing the target-chart center and one actual wall corner eliminates the selected-stress obstruction. A layer-peeling proof rules out every nonnegative equilibrium on the punctured chart plus the five retained corner guards, so a strict nonaffine direction exists. Curvature opens every remaining noncorner wall guard. The resulting `N-1`-site packet excludes canonical sources with `z<=N-2` interior points in this wall class; its Oler area threshold leaves `z=N-1` or `N`. Audits.77--78 subsequently exclude the complete specified fixed-profile wall family for every fixed `M>=4`. Arbitrary wall extraction remains open.

Audit.73 gives an exact equality regression for audit.71: every fixed `M>=3` and every permutation of the radial rates has an explicit unit-separated sorting packet with `E_r=12C` on every nested ring. Deleting the center still leaves an actual unit triangle. This rules out a strict energy-gap deduction from the physical lower constraints and six guards alone; it does not refute a stronger triangle-free hypothesis and is not a global-source counterexample. Audit.78 separately promotes the fixed-profile highest-population classification; arbitrary-wall extraction remains open.

Audit.74 promotes the Oler equality limit for the `z=N` population: every sequence of placements in the specified six-sided curved wall converges along a subsequence to the complete triangular-lattice chart as `theta` tends to zero. This is a limiting reduction only. Audit.78 supplies the separate finite classification and triangle conclusion for each fixed profile.

Audit.75 promotes `C-ONE-VACANCY-FLAT-PINNING-RATE` for auxiliary triangular-lattice charts: for every interior vacancy and every `M>=2`, pinning the full boundary gives a strictly positive equilibrium and full moving-column rank, hence a zero static cone and local flat-wall isolation. Feasible placements converging to such a chart under an `O(theta)` boundary perturbation move by `O(theta)` and have bounded subsequential first velocities. The theorem does not extract an arbitrary source as a vacancy chart or supply a finite exchange.

Audit.77 extends the one-deficit strict packet and full-source exclusion to every fixed `M>=4` and every fixed strictly decreasing symmetric profile, for all sufficiently small positive curvature. When `z<=N-1`, the exchange deletes all `z` strict-interior labels, including any interior contact isolates, and one wall vertex, then inserts `z+1` packet points; every source point outside the wall is retained. Interior isolate identities are not preserved. Audit.78 classifies the remaining `z=N` cloud for every fixed `M>=3` as an exact sorting packet with a unit triangle. Thus the specified fixed-wall source class is exhausted for `M>=4`; for `M=3`, the highest population is classified but lower populations remain. Arbitrary wall extraction remains open.

Audit.79 extracts the full active-row rank used in the maximum-population classification and proves an explicit first correction for the descending principal radial-chain stress. Its diagonal weak-row coefficient has a fixed-profile lower bound; the exact stress continuation is per fixed M/profile, with no uniform onset. The result does not change the arbitrary-wall extraction obligation.

Audit.80 gives an explicit joint window for the symmetric arithmetic profile `a_h=M-1-2h`: for every `M>=4` and `0<theta<=1/(100*M^3)`, it proves the one-deficit strict packet, Oler cap `z<=N`, and full-source exclusion when `z<=N-1`. This uses the same exchange: interior isolates are included in `z` and replaced as part of the full interior cloud, while all source points outside the wall remain. Audit.107 adds a common growing-M classification of the remaining `z=N` case for `0<theta<=c_*M^-40`, with one universal positive `c_*`, and hence excludes the entire specified arithmetic-wall class on that narrower interval. Arbitrary wall extraction remains open.

Audit.81 extends Oler localization and the full-chart `O(theta)` pinning rate to any fixed C2 convex six-sided unit-wall family tending to `H_M`. It proves that a maximal `z=N` cloud arbitrarily near zero curvature requires all six strictly ordered first side-rate profiles to match after one common infinitesimal rotation. A mismatch gives a family-dependent `z<=N-1` cap, but no packet or source-level wall extraction. C-II remains open.

Audit.82 proves that area-form Oler slack at most `1/1000` forces an arbitrary convex unit-wall packing to have a developed triangular-lattice chart, with explicit edge/angle errors and displacement at most `60*b*sigma`. For the specified arithmetic six-wall, all feasible `z=N` clouds at `0<theta<=1/(10000*M^3)` lie within `1000*M^4*theta` of `P_M`. Audit.107 turns this into an exact sorting-packet classification on `0<theta<=c_*M^-40`, uniformly for `M>=4`, and proves the corresponding canonical fixed-wall exclusion. It still does not extract such a wall from an arbitrary source; C-II remains open.

Audit.153 adds `C-ONE-DEFICIT-DIFFUSE-STRAIN-NONGLOBAL-REGRESSION`: an explicit triangle-free oblique grid has exactly one point's area-form Oler allowance, is saturated against inserting a point without moving its labels, and has increasingly equilateral Delaunay triangles. The entire source is nevertheless beaten by an equal-cardinality square grid of smaller diameter. This is a parallelogram-wall nonglobal regression; it supplies no curved-wall or canonical-source conclusion.

Audit.83 independently audits the face-coupled quarter-fold route. Face closure integrates to an exact finite contact-preserving fold; the canonical stress yields strict weighted diameter currency for a nonaffine state when `D>=9`, but only an endpoint keeping old contacts unit, old noncontacts strictly above one, old diameters at most D, and old nondiameters below D would contradict the lexicographic minimum. Exact two-hop compatibility relations and convex-face transition classification restrict sign states. Hexagon and rhombic-grid regressions reject shortcuts through weighted mean decrease or old-diameter shortening alone. The existence of a nonaffine fully feasible endpoint remains open.

Audit.222 first promoted C-QUARTER-FOLD-MINIMUM-FACE-SIZE under a full endpoint retaining every other label. Audit.229 broadens the same result to boundary-local unit separation and triangle-freeness: every nonconstant face fold on a simple convex actual face with at least five sides has four transition arcs of length at least two, so a nonconstant eight-face has pattern `2,2,2,2`; faces of size five through seven must be constant. This supplies no outside-label feasibility or full integrated endpoint. See the [Audit.229 independent audit](../../audits/target_c_minimum_face_size_local_endpoint_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md); the [original Audit.222 audit](../../audits/target_c_quarter_fold_minimum_face_size_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md) remains historical evidence.

Audit.223 promotes C-QUARTER-FOLD-EIGHT-FACE-AREA-LOWER-BOUND: an actual convex eight-sided contact face admitting a nonconstant face-closed quarter-fold with a full unit-separated, triangle-free endpoint has area strictly greater than 2sqrt(3). Audit.226 proves the infimum in this finite face class is exactly 2sqrt(3), with no uniform positive gap; its sharpness sequence is strictly nonglobal. No source-level population or face extraction follows. C-II remains open. See the [area audit](../../audits/target_c_quarter_fold_eight_face_area_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md) and [sharpness audit](../../audits/target_c_quarter_fold_eight_face_area_sharpness_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

Audit.224 first promoted C-QUARTER-FOLD-TRANSFORMED-EIGHT-FACE-HULL-COVER under a full endpoint retaining all other labels. Audit.228 broadens it to boundary-local unit separation and triangle-freeness for the pattern with four two-edge arcs, deriving the needed turn window directly and removing the area-bound dependency. Audit.229 supplies that pattern under the same local hypotheses for every nonconstant eight-face. The transformed boundary hull is therefore covered by closed unit disks for any such face; if either arc pair bows, the cover is strict everywhere. Thus it has no strict isolate slot and, in the bowed case, no unit-separated insertion. This does not supply outside capacity or an integrated endpoint. See the [Audit.228 independent audit](../../audits/target_c_local_quarter_fold_hull_cover_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md) and [Audit.229 independent audit](../../audits/target_c_minimum_face_size_local_endpoint_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md); the [original Audit.224 audit](../../audits/target_c_quarter_fold_transformed_hull_cover_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md) remains historical evidence.

Audit.225 promotes C-PARALLELOGRAM-UNIT-GUARD-CENTER-BOUND: the weak unit-clear set of any parallelogram with side lengths at most sqrt(3) is contained in the closed radius-1/2 disk about its center; the strict-clear set lies in the open disk and has diameter below one. It also promotes C-QUARTER-FOLD-EIGHT-FACE-ISOLATE-BOUND: an actual bounded convex eight-sided contact face with a nonconstant boundary fold that retains every other source label and has a full unit-separated, triangle-free endpoint contains at most one contact-isolated source point. This does not bound the number of such faces or supply outside refill capacity, and its retention hypothesis does not follow from an integrated endpoint that relocates isolates. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_quarter_fold_eight_face_isolate_bound_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

Audit.227 promotes C-INTEGRATED-FOLD-RIGID-FACE-ISOLATE-TRANSPORT under explicit endpoint and embedding hypotheses. Isolates in constant-sign bounded faces can be transported rigidly; a unit-edge crossing argument gives separation above sqrt(3) from exterior core vertices and isolates in other faces, while convex coefficients give squared upper margin below D^2-1. This works for simple nonconvex faces, but does not establish the embedding or remaining isolate ledger. C-II remains open. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_rigid_face_isolate_transport_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

Audit.230 promotes `C-QUARTER-FOLD-OUTER-ISOLATE-PAYMENT`. Under an exact-contact-graph, unit-separated endpoint with the corresponding actual face embedding, the outside-region isolate population must increase by at least the old isolate population in nonconstant convex eight-faces. This is a count statement, not a claim about which labels move outside. Constant-sign faces cannot gain net isolate population beyond their old count, by the canonical strict wall-packet bound. The result allows any number of old isolates per folded face; the earlier fixed-other-label one-isolate theorem is not used. It gives no outer capacity bound, endpoint, or C-II closure. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_quarter_fold_outer_isolate_payment_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).


Audit.61 reduces a sufficiently small O–O motion to
\[
\boxed{
\text{old diameter constraints}
+\ |J|\le d_H\text{ inactive upper exceptions}
+\ \text{unary rhombus tests}
+\ \text{outer-face near-unit pairs}.
}
\]

Audit.62 makes the remaining near-unit geometry substantially more ordered.

Canonical \`C-LOADED-NEAR-UNIT-ADJACENCY\` gives
\[
\tau_D=\frac{3D}{\sqrt{3D^2+4}+1}
\]
and proves that every nonconsecutive loaded pair \(a,b\) with \(|ab|<D\) satisfies
\[
|ab|\ge\tau_D.
\]
For \(D\ge6\), every loaded pair of length at most \(13/12\) is therefore a loaded-hull side. The loaded near-unit conflict graph is a subgraph of the loaded hull cycle and has maximum degree two.

Canonical \`C-LOADED-HULL-CAP-BUDGET\` controls geometry outside and near the loaded hull. With
\[
W=\operatorname{per}(\operatorname{conv}A)-q,
\]
occupied loaded-hull caps are at most \(W\), their total outer/base length is at most \(2W\), short core-to-exterior attachments are supported on at most \(68W\) core vertices, the nonloaded \(1/2\)-boundary layer inside the loaded hull has size at most \(12W\), and extreme source vertices outside the loaded set are at most \(W\). Deep loaded-hull interior points have an exact \(D-1/2\) upper margin.

Since canonical full-source geometry gives
\[
\operatorname{per}(\operatorname{conv}A)<\pi D,
\]
every subsequence with \(q/D\to\pi\) has \(W=o(D)\). Thus loaded-hull cap/exterior blockers are sublinear in the terminal regime.

Audit.63 narrows the live full-core branch: when q/D -> pi and u_B=o(D), no linear O--O stopped-train bank remains, and distinct narrow-window binary loaded witnesses number at most u_B. Any remaining linear obstruction must use face-coupled outer-to-bounded or bounded-to-bounded trains, unary rhombus tests, or the at most d_H inactive upper exceptions, while meeting all source lower and actual-diameter upper constraints.

The one-vertex shield retains contact isolates and excludes strict one-site activation in canonical unit hexagons; it does not yield general packet capacity.

Audit.84 promotes the full-source hull-augmented cell structure and exact single-cell strict capacity. For a face with one source-hull arc and a unit path, a replacement clear of the path is strictly separated from every retained point outside the cell, including isolates. Actual loaded tips are acute; at tip angles at most `pi/3` and internal wall angles at most `2pi/3`, deleting separated guards does not enlarge the strict region. The exact capacity remains the full assigned isolate count `|S_Q|`, so this creates no spare site or macroscopic-cap closure. The audit also records a shared-loaded-tip cross-collision regression; paired gluing with four distinct separated tips remains a separate unaudited claim. See the [independent Audit.84](../../audits/target_c_exterior_hull_cell_and_tip_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.85 now promotes that paired geometry only under the explicit finite hypothesis of four distinct extreme loaded tips pairwise at least four apart. The full-source shield checks retained isolates, and the two cell packets are strictly separated even with weak wall guards. If packets meet the exact cardinality, triangle-free, and contact-budget conditions, the actual diameter-pair endpoint excludes the exchange. In particular, the sum of the two strict guarded capacities is at most the two assigned isolate counts plus three. The unreviewed antipodal cap-pair route does not establish the four-tip hypothesis, so no large-source cap conclusion follows. See the [independent Audit.85](../../audits/target_c_exterior_paired_cell_gluing_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.86 promotes the finite positive-circuit transversal characterization for strict-negative contact directions and records two exact covector regressions. The source report's residual-cage release/refill is valid but superseded for its stated strict wall-safe packets: `kappa>=1` makes its packet condition pay at least one extra site, which the canonical contact-budget endpoint already excludes without a source motion. The exact topology-only and positive-spanning deletion regressions are in the [independent Audit.86](../../audits/target_c_residual_cage_release_refill_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.87 promotes the finite KKT strict-motion alternative and the canonical loaded-diameter-support transversal. The support criterion is on the full normalized source stress polytope; contact-isolated points stay retained, while the strict motion is only for the reduced source. The costs satisfy `kappa_open<=kappa(H,G)<=tau_*<=s_Z+1`, but no useful geometric bound, finite pivot, refill, or cardinality restoration is proved. The exact nonlinear regression refutes equality between first-order and finite-release deletion costs in a smooth abstract chart only. See the [independent Audit.87](../../audits/target_c_contact_opening_transversal_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.88 promotes a full-source shield for any simple source-vertex polygon with side lengths at most `sqrt(3)`. Wall-safe sites are strictly separated from every old source point outside the polygon; packets in pairwise interior-disjoint walls are jointly unit-separated. A wall-safe point in an `n`-edge polygon requires maximum side length `>2 sin(pi/n)`, giving a fixed positive displacement threshold for opening a unit pentagon. A rational rectangle regression makes the `sqrt(3)` cutoff sharp. The geometry does not certify a feasible motion or spare capacity. See the [independent Audit.88](../../audits/target_c_noncontact_wall_shield_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.89 promotes the finite actual-face depth-population bound from `C-GLOBAL-FACE-RETURN-QUOTAS` and the subquadratic-core macroscopic-face corollary. An area-`Omega(D^2)` bounded physical face has linear perimeter and, for each fixed `eta>0`, at most half plus `o(D)` boundary vertices at distance at least `eta D` from actual loaded endpoints. This leaves outer pockets, exterior caps, quadratic-core sequences, and the capacity/finite-competitor endpoint open. See the [independent Audit.89](../../audits/target_c_macroscopic_face_return_depth_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.90 promotes the unloaded KKT stress transversal: if no normalized stress is supported entirely on the loaded endpoints, at most `s_Z+1` unloaded positive-contact vertices can be deleted to eliminate every stress and obtain a strict first motion on the reduced source. In the full-core loaded-chord regime only, any surviving loaded-only stress uses every loaded-hull side and has positive diameter support at every loaded vertex; this gives a transversal with any prescribed loaded endpoint and at most `s_Z` unloaded vertices. An exact weighted-work regression blocks opening all deleted contacts along that same reduced descent. None of these first-order statements supplies packet capacity or finite activation. See the [independent Audit.90](../../audits/target_c_unloaded_stress_transversal_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.91 promotes a finite affine-lattice vertex count, an exact-lattice attachment remainder bound, and two conditional approximate-lattice cover obstructions. In either canonical branch, a fixed number of uniformly conditioned lattices with vanishing physical positional error cannot cover all but `o(D)` loaded endpoints; in the full-core near-disk branch the finite radial-arc estimate also forces `q=o(D)`. The exact-lattice component application leaves at least `D-O(D^(8/9))` loaded endpoints unused or attached to nonlattice components. These conditions are not derived from phase coherence; no packet or C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.91](../../audits/target_c_loaded_attachment_lattice_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.92 promotes the exact bent-grid regression `C-BENT-GRID-REDUCED-POSITIONAL-TRANSPORT-REGRESSION`. Small phase spread, `O(D)` total angular and area excess, positive pinned equilibrium, and full pinned rank do not by themselves give a vanishing single-lattice positional approximation of the physical grain. The construction's pins are not actual diameter endpoints, so this does not refute the conditional cover claims of Audit.91 for the canonical loaded set, and it leaves bounded multiple-lattice covers open. The finite-move and capacity endpoint is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.92](../../audits/target_c_bent_grid_positional_transport_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.93 promotes `C-PENTAGON-CAPPED-LOCKED-TRAIN-BANK-REGRESSION`. An explicit near-triangular parallelogram grid can have quadratic physical train crossings, linearly many long trains, and disjoint direction-frustrated pentagon terminal walls, yet the exact fixed-container capacity leaves no positive aggregate surplus for any bank of wall-safe packets inside actual contact cycles, including composite walls. This uses the general finite-source wall shield and the short-pentagon zero-capacity theorem. The boundary pins are not certified actual diameter endpoints and the source is nonglobal; the result only blocks a geometry-only fixed-wall surplus implication. It does not change the active loaded-return/movable-wall finite-competitor endpoint. See the [independent Audit.93](../../audits/target_c_pentagon_capped_locked_train_bank_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.94 promotes `C-PAIRED-CAP-SPOKE-NONGLOBAL-REGRESSION`. The explicit six-spoke source has actual loaded endpoints and positive KKT equilibrium, six antipodal exterior caps with `Theta(D^2)` actual isolates and `O(D)` Oler allowance, and a `3/5` isolate cover of each depth-one cap. A discrete guard injection gives a same-cardinality unit-separated competitor of diameter `D-1/2`. The source fails canonical mixed-rigidity and loaded-diameter density, so the regression blocks deriving canonicality or global optimality from those reduced cap features; it does not prove the macroscopic-cap extraction for canonical minimizers. See the [independent Audit.94](../../audits/target_c_paired_cap_spoke_global_comparison_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.95 adds two exact local common-angle regressions. A single regular-hexagon face with six independent side bits has a non-Horn closure relation, even after existential Horn auxiliaries; this does not classify arbitrary train-network relations. In a second finite example, the alternating pattern shortens every core pair below the old core diameter but increases the actual diameter pair to a retained isolate. Common-angle proposals must therefore respect the complete source pair list. Neither example supplies a canonical source or a C-II closure. See the [independent Audit.95](../../audits/target_c_common_angle_hexagon_and_isolate_regressions_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

The regular-shell barrier payment and strict sub-diameter whole-source competitor are recorded in the [Audit.65 independent audit](../../audits/target_c_odd_shell_barrier_payment_independent_audit_2026-10-06.md). They constrain that explicit shell family and do not close the macroscopic-container branch.

The [Audit.66 collective wall audit](../../audits/target_c_collective_wall_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-06.md) retains exterior isolates under separated multi-vertex deletions, but proves no packet capacity for large chambers.

The [Audit.67 curved-wall audit](../../audits/target_c_curved_wall_independent_audit_2026-10-06.md) excludes one explicit wall-plus-grid chamber under a full-source clearance hypothesis and corrects an isolate-only blocker claim.

The [Audit.68 near-triangular wall audit](../../audits/target_c_near_triangular_curved_wall_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-06.md) extends this to arbitrary old interior populations under the strict Oler-area threshold; it does not prove arbitrary chamber extraction.

The [Audit.69 uneven-wall audit](../../audits/target_c_uneven_wall_packet_independent_audit_2026-10-06.md) carries the exact capacity exchange to independently bent opposite side pairs and records the corner-root failure that motivates its middle bank.

The [Audit.70 corner-barrier audit](../../audits/target_c_three_direction_corner_barrier_independent_audit_2026-10-06.md) records the exact lower-constraint obstruction to the complete triangular-lattice affine packet when all corner guards remain.

The [Audit.71 nested-stress audit](../../audits/target_c_nested_hexagon_packet_stress_independent_audit_2026-10-06.md) proves the selected ring-and-spoke stress identities and their per-ring second-order energy demand. It does not establish an energy upper bound or a source-level packet.

The [Audit.72 one-corner packet audit](../../audits/target_c_one_corner_one_vacancy_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) proves the punctured-chart strict packet, the full-source contact-loss exchange for `z<=N-2`, and the conditional Oler residual `{N-1,N}`. It does not extract the wall or close C-II.

The [Audit.73 sorted-rate energy audit](../../audits/target_c_sorted_rate_energy_equality_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) promotes only the finite equality regression. Its center-deleted packet still contains triangles; the fixed-profile highest-population classification is audited separately in Audit.78.

The [Audit.74 Oler-limit audit](../../audits/target_c_six_sided_wall_oler_high_population_limit_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) fixes the subsequential limit of every `z=N` cloud at `P_(M-1)`. Audit.78 separately proves a finite sorting classification and a unit triangle for each fixed profile.

The [Audit.75 flat vacancy pinning/rate audit](../../audits/target_c_one_vacancy_flat_pinning_rate_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) gives a zero-cone and `O(theta)` localization theorem for every auxiliary one-vacancy lattice chart. It leaves the curved-wall first-order system, arbitrary-source extraction, and finite packet question open.

The [Audit.76 one-deficit root-packet audit](../../audits/target_c_one_deficit_root_packet_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) promotes five exact-curvature cases. Its M4 all-small-curvature result is subsumed by [Audit.77](../../audits/target_c_one_deficit_sector_peeling_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md), which proves the all-fixed-profile `M>=4` theorem. The [independent isolate-accounting follow-up](../../audits/target_c_one_deficit_isolate_accounting_independent_audit_2026-10-10.md) reconstructs the full-source `z<=N-1` exchange and confirms that interior isolates are counted and removed. Neither Audit.76 nor Audit.77 extracts the wall from a canonical source.

The [Audit.78 highest-population classification audit](../../audits/target_c_profile_wall_sorting_classification_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) proves that every maximum-population fixed-profile wall cloud is a sorting packet with a unit triangle. Together with Audit.77 this exhausts the specified fixed-wall class for every fixed `M>=4`; no arbitrary-wall extraction or C-II closure follows.

The [Audit.79 active-row and stress-correction audit](../../audits/target_c_profile_first_stress_correction_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) extracts the full active-row rank lemma and gives the all-M explicit first correction for the descending principal radial-chain stress. It strengthens the local stress bridge with no common onset in M/profile and does not change the arbitrary-wall C-II obligation.

The [Audit.80 arithmetic-profile window audit](../../audits/target_c_arithmetic_one_deficit_uniform_window_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) makes the one-deficit packet and Oler source-population bounds explicit uniformly in `M` on `0<theta<=1/(100*M^3)` for `a_h=M-1-2h`. It excludes only `z<=N-1`; it does not uniformly classify `z=N` or extract the wall from an arbitrary source.

The [Audit.81 general first-profile audit](../../audits/target_c_general_six_wall_first_profile_compatibility_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) proves the fixed-family Oler limit, full-chart pinning rate, and matching of the six first boundary-rate profiles under maximal population. A mismatch lowers the wall population by one, without supplying the required strict packet or arbitrary-source extraction.

The [Audit.82 effective-chart audit](../../audits/target_c_effective_small_slack_unit_wall_chart_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) promotes the low-Oler-slack triangular-lattice development and the uniform `P_M` localization on the arithmetic six-wall. It gives no finite sorting classification, triangle forcing, or arbitrary-wall source extraction.

Audit.107 promotes `C-ARITHMETIC-SIX-WALL-POLYNOMIAL-MAXIMUM-CLASSIFICATION`: for every `M>=4` and `0<theta<=c_*M^-40`, with one universal `c_*>0`, each maximal cloud in the specified arithmetic wall is an exact sorting packet and contains a unit triangle. `C-ARITHMETIC-SIX-WALL-POLYNOMIAL-SOURCE-EXCLUSION` combines this with Audit.80 to exclude canonical triangle-free sources containing that wall across the same joint growing-M interval. In the `z<=N-1` exchange, every source point outside the wall is retained, while all strict-interior labels, including any contact isolates, are counted in `z` and replaced together with one wall vertex. This makes no label-retention claim for interior isolates. The result supplies no arbitrary-source extraction; C-II stays open. See the [independent Audit.107](../../audits/target_c_uniform_polynomial_wall_classification_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

The [Audit.83 face-coupled quarter-fold audit](../../audits/target_c_face_coupled_quarter_fold_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) promotes exact fold integration, conditional stress currency, source-realized sign constraints, and convex-face transition classification, together with three finite regressions. It supplies no universally feasible nonaffine state or C-II closure.

Audit.64 requires every finite exchange to count all inserted-to-retained contacts and actual diameter pairs. With strict upper slack, one or two new unit contacts can be opened, so a packet that removes an old contact must create at least three new contacts unless it also removes an actual diameter pair or strictly improves diameter.

The audit.61 regressions remain binding:

- arbitrarily small contact-preserving motion can still break a unary rhombus diagonal;
- a source path forest can become cyclic after contraction to owner labels.

The live full-core target is now a source-realized **face-coupled finite-move theorem**. A linear O--O train supply is unavailable in the terminal q/D -> pi, u_B=o(D) regime, and narrow-window binary loaded witnesses cost at most u_B. The remaining endpoint must use outer-to-bounded or bounded-to-bounded trains, explicit unary rhombus tests, or at most d_H inactive upper exceptions, while meeting every full-source lower and actual-diameter upper constraint.

The macroscopic-container branch remains separate. Audit.89 proves a finite actual-contact-face depth-population bound from canonical loaded-return quotas and, when `|V|=o(D^2)`, forces at least half of any area-`Omega(D^2)` bounded physical face boundary to lie within every fixed macroscopic scale of actual loaded endpoints. Audit.108 adds a common closed-unit-covering Delaunay completion for arbitrary convex polygon crops: Oler slack `S`, boundary-strip population `B<=20p/pi+25`, triangle excess `Sigma<=sqrt(3)S/2+7sqrt(3)B`, and at most `73B+(12/delta+450)Sigma` exceptional vertices. For `S,p=O(D)` these are `O(D)` geometric bookkeeping bounds. They do not identify Delaunay edges with source contacts, create a spare site, or give a source-realized finite move. The canonical inactive-chamber budget gives `m<=13*pi*D`; outer pockets, exterior caps, packet capacity, and the full-source finite-competitor endpoint remain open. See the [independent return-depth audit](../../audits/target_c_macroscopic_face_return_depth_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) and [Audit.108](../../audits/target_c_coupled_delaunay_container_transfer_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.110 promotes `C-EXTERIOR-STATIC-PACKET-BARRIER`: in the canonical `D>=9`, `C=C_*`, `H=H_*` source, an equal-cardinality packet outside the interior of the positive-contact hull, with every packet-involving pair in `[1,D)`, cannot replace deleted labels when it deletes an old contact or covers every old diameter pair. A finite motion opens every new contact, retaining contact isolates as moving variables; contact minimality or globality then gives the contradiction. Boundary packet points and initially triangular packets are allowed. Audit.109's one-site theorem is a direct special case and is superseded. The result supplies no exterior packet, interior capacity, or moving-core competitor; C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.110](../../audits/target_c_exterior_static_packet_barrier_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.111 promotes the finite diameter-disk chord threshold for an occupied loaded-hull cap and widens `C-LOADED-ONLY-STRESS-CYCLE-OBSTRUCTION` to all finite unit-separated sources with D >= 6, a two-dimensional actual loaded hull, and a normalized nonnegative equilibrium stress. In a canonical C-II source, an occupied cap forces the loaded-only stress face empty; the existing unloaded transversal then gives a reduced-cardinality strict diameter descent retaining contact isolates. This gives no refill, packet capacity, or same-cardinality competitor. See the [independent Audit.111](../../audits/target_c_loaded_only_stress_scope_extension_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.112 promotes `C-SEPARATED-PACKET-RELEASE-OBSTRUCTION`. A finite replacement packet partitioned into pieces strictly separated from all retained labels can be moved to open every new contact when distinct pieces have strict cross-separation and every inserted-involving upper distance is strict. This gives a contact-minimality contradiction after deleting an old contact without the secondary diameter-pair normalization; deleting every old diameter pair gives a strict sub-D competitor. It provides no packet existence or capacity and does not change the C-II endpoint. See the [independent report](../../audits/target_c_separated_packet_release_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) and [audit](../../audits/target_c_separated_packet_release_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.113 promotes `C-ONE-GUARD-EXTERIOR-CAP-SATURATION`: for an actual loaded-hull cap with positive-contact core inside the loaded hull, omitting one contact-active guard leaves exactly the old cap-isolate population of weakly unit-clear interior sites. Exact anchors are permitted because Audit.112 opens packet contacts; the old cap isolates give the matching lower bound by a small inward move. It creates no surplus and does not settle paired-cap geometry. See the [independent report](../../audits/target_c_one_guard_exterior_cap_saturation_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) and [audit](../../audits/target_c_one_guard_exterior_cap_saturation_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.114 promotes `C-OO-ORIGINAL-FAMILY-OWNER-CAPACITY`: for a fixed, subfamily-stable disjoint O–O proposal family satisfying the canonical singleton and lower-witness hypotheses, the original family size is at most `2m_B+3(x_B+Z_out)+2d_H`; the Type-II version is at most `147(x_B+m_B)+2d_H`. The `2d_H` charge counts owners touched by the fixed exceptional upper-pair set. This is not a simultaneous-move or source-extraction theorem. See the [independent Audit.114](../../audits/target_c_original_family_outer_owner_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.115 promotes `C-ANTIPODAL-EXTERIOR-CAP-PAIRING`. Each macroscopic exterior cap in a canonical C-II sequence has a distinct opposite cap with the same leading area and actual isolate population, and their actual isolate hulls have antipodal normalized limits. For fixed `epsilon`, at most `pi/(4 epsilon)` caps have area at least `epsilon D^2`; the smaller caps contain at most `(sqrt(pi epsilon)/a_0)D^2+O(D)` exterior isolates. Audit.183 later derives separated tips for one such cap pair and an upper bound on their combined strict guarded-region capacity. A paired-cap release, a packet, and a strict sub-D source remain open. See the [independent Audit.115](../../audits/target_c_antipodal_exterior_cap_pairing_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) and [Audit.183](../../audits/target_c_macroscopic_antipodal_cap_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

Audit.183 promotes `C-MACROSCOPIC-ANTIPODAL-CAP-PAIR-STRICT-CAPACITY`. For any fixed-positive-area exterior cap, its Audit.115 opposite partner has four actual loaded cell tips separated by at least `gamma D`; the canonical force-hull inradius floor prevents cross-pair collapse. Audit.85 therefore gives `M_1+M_2<=|S_1|+|S_2|+3` for the two strict guarded regions after omitting those four tips. The paired cells cannot supply a net strict-capacity surplus of four. This is a fixed-wall capacity ceiling, not a packet or source move; C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.183](../../audits/target_c_macroscopic_antipodal_cap_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

Audit.184 promotes `C-ACUTE-BINARY-SINK-WITNESS-DEBT`, `C-BINARY-SINK-OWNER-RUN-TRADEOFF`, `C-LOADED-BINARY-SINK-DOUBLE-RECURRENCE`, and `C-LOADED-SINK-TRAIN-WITNESS-TRADEOFF`. The acute pair identity makes the `m+1` packet witnesses physically distinct; loaded-loaded witnesses are distinct actual loaded-hull sides. The resulting run lower bounds combine with Audit.127's run upper bound only for pairwise-disjoint proper O--O crosscuts. In the canonical `T_OO=o(D)` regime this excludes linear owner mass in the stated all-loaded sink family, but does not charge upstream implication basins or establish a finite source move. C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.184](../../audits/target_c_acute_binary_sink_run_payment_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

Audit.185 promotes `C-BINARY-SINK-UNLOADED-WITNESS-OUTER-CAP` and `C-BINARY-SINK-OUTER-EXCESS-TRADEOFF`. Distinct unloaded-endpoint witnesses in the post-dispatch acute binary sink system number at most `6(b-q+Z_out)`: the source near-unit graph has degree at most six, the loaded vertices use `q` distinguished outer-walk occurrences, and outer contact isolates remain in `Z_out`. Thus sink count is at most that outer term plus loaded-run excess, and single-run sinks are bounded by the outer term alone. No asymptotic bound on the outer term or run excess follows; C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.185](../../audits/target_c_unloaded_sink_outer_occurrence_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

Audit.116 promotes `C-COMMON-ANGLE-ALGEBRAIC-FULL-SOURCE-PAIR-TEST`. For one fixed, normalized closed train-bit potential on a connected contact component, every pair in the full source, including pairs involving contact isolates and other fixed components, has an exact univariate quadratic distance-change test. With algebraic coordinates, root isolation decides whether that specified path has an endpoint satisfying all lower, upper, and old-diameter constraints. This supplies no feasible pattern or endpoint for a canonical source and leaves the active C-II obligation unchanged. See the [independent Audit.116](../../audits/target_c_common_angle_full_source_pair_test_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.117 promotes `C-MIXED-CORE-FINITE-RIGIDITY`: under the canonical positive-semidefinite KKT stress and the core-scoped ZRF/ZF theorem, any finite-dimensional placement preserving every actual contact and diameter length on the nonisolated core is an isometric affine image of that core. This finite rigidity statement excludes contact isolates and does not extend to diameter upper inequalities or a feasible full-source move. See the [independent Audit.117](../../audits/target_c_finite_mixed_core_rigidity_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.118 promotes `C-MIXED-CORE-THREE-VERTEX-CONNECTIVITY`, `C-TWO-PUNCTURE-LOADED-ANCHORING`, and `C-MIXED-CORE-ONE-ROW-REDUNDANCY`: the full actual contact/diameter graph on the nonisolated core is 3-vertex-connected, deleting at most two core labels leaves every contact component incident to an actual diameter endpoint, and any one row can be removed from the mixed rigidity matrix without reducing its rank. The unique-crossing-contact corollary is included. These are core graph/matrix facts; they do not locate a physical source cut or change the C-II frontier. See the [independent Audit.118](../../audits/target_c_mixed_core_connectivity_and_two_puncture_anchoring_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.119 promotes `C-DIAMETER-LEAF-CONTACT-INCIDENCE`: with `q` loaded diameter endpoints and diameter deficiency `d_H`, the exact full-source contact incidence satisfies `t_BI+2e_BB >= q+ell_H >= q+2d_H`. Three-connectivity pays a second contact at every diameter leaf; Woodall pays two leaves per caterpillar component. This is an incidence count only: it does not assign contacts to distinct faces or trains and leaves the active C-II endpoint unchanged. See the [independent Audit.119](../../audits/target_c_diameter_leaf_contact_incidence_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.120 promotes `C-FINITE-CONTACT-ISOMETRY-DIAMETER-GAIN` and `C-FINITE-CONTACT-ISOMETRY-DIAMETER-REPLACEMENT`. On the nonisolated core, a nonaffine contact-isometry has strictly positive weighted old-diameter shortening; a full-source unit-separated placement with the same exact contact graph and diameter at most `D` must replace every shortened diameter pair by at least as many new diameter pairs. The spectral gap is source-dependent, and the corollary counts all pairs involving contact isolates. It supplies no feasible endpoint or strict sub-`D` competitor, so C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.120](../../audits/target_c_contact_isometry_energy_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.121 promotes `C-NORMALIZED-CONTACT-ISOMETRY-INTERPOLATION-THRESHOLD`, `C-CONTACT-ISOMETRY-SPARSE-STRESS-BARRIER`, and `C-TWO-VALUED-SLAB-INTERPOLATION-BLOCKER`. Contact-restored straight interpolation has exact old-core contact and diameter tests, with the necessary parameter threshold `t>A_*/(A_*+G)`; moving only a small fraction of contact stress mass forces `t` close to one. A two-valued slab endpoint with a nonshortened old diameter cannot be rescued by this normalized interpolation. This does not check inactive source pairs, move contact isolates, or supply the endpoint. C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.121](../../audits/target_c_normalized_contact_isometry_interpolation_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.122 records two exact guardrails from PR #624: a rank-five abstract bar framework can hinge across a two-vertex cut, and a regular unit heptagon with positive PSD equilibrium stress plus mixed infinitesimal rigidity still has a contact-preserving strict-diameter competitor when new contacts are allowed. The heptagon is not globally optimal and its replacement adds contacts, so neither regression challenges the canonical finite contact-isometry theorem. C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.122](../../audits/target_c_finite_hinge_and_heptagon_stress_regressions_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.123 promotes a rational regression family with positive pinned equilibrium, full pinned contact rigidity, `O(D)` exceptional-face area, and persistent face-defect tail `T_M/D -> 2` for every fixed cutoff. The sources are nonglobal and only four pins are actual diameter endpoints; this rejects local pinned-data closures without challenging canonical full-source Type-II claims. C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.123](../../audits/target_c_positive_pinned_face_tail_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.124 promotes the global monotone positive-contact route-debit bank. For every projection direction, strict successors along actual positive-contact edges terminate at actual loaded attachments; the slow-vertex debit is bounded by one Section-123 augmented-cell payment, and route excess can be summed across routes with disjoint unloaded tails. Two exact local regressions show that raw `Theta(D)` route length is not face-defect currency and that attachment-triangle debit cannot be omitted. The separate attachment-gap claim remains unaudited, and the high-cap use keeps its proof-only co-location premise. No positive excess demand, bounded overlap, source replacement, or C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.124](../../audits/target_c_monotone_route_debit_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.96 adds the exact capped-wall actual-diameter escape regression. Under an explicit full-source oblique-grid and pentagon-cap contact hypothesis, the opposite grid corners are the unique actual diameter pair, yet a small common-angle motion of the whole packet preserves every contact and strictly lowers its actual diameter. The two finite source realizations are triangle-free and pass all-pair interval checks. This is a scoped nonglobal result, not a canonical-source extraction or a motion through arbitrary retained exterior points; the C-II endpoint is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.96](../../audits/target_c_capped_wall_actual_diameter_escape_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.97 promotes the necessary source-dependent half-angle thresholds for a nonconstant integrated common-angle train pattern on a canonical C-II core with `D>=9`. Positive and negative diameter transverse terms follow from relative-interior stress balance and mixed rigidity. Every old diameter upper bound requires the angle to clear the corresponding positive `B/A` or `-B/A` maximum when its denominators are positive; a wrong-sign denominator rules out that direction. This is not a feasible-endpoint theorem, gives no uniform threshold, and leaves every retained isolate pair in the full endpoint check. The active C-II obligation does not change. See the [independent Audit.97 report](../../audits/target_c_common_angle_active_diameter_amplitude_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.98 promotes the pentagon component bit-collapse theorem. Face closure on an actual physical triangle-free unit pentagon forces all five side bits to agree; shared complete trains propagate the bit across each connected pentagon component. Reciprocal sparsity gives at least `f+6` outside terminal incidences for a component of `f>=2` faces, but all those occurrences carry the same bit. No existence of a large physical component or linear suppressed-proposal count is proved. See the [independent Audit.98](../../audits/target_c_pentagon_train_component_bit_collapse_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.99 promotes `C-ZERO-VALID-PINNED-CLOSURE`: for any finite zero-valid 2-CNF formula in implication/negative-unit/negative-pair form, pinned satisfiability is exactly forward closure avoiding unary/false pins and forbidden pairs. Failure has a directed path to a forbidden variable or two directed paths to a forbidden pair. The unpinned nonzero criterion is the existence of an eligible sink SCC. This gives a reusable exact certificate for finite-move obstruction systems. It does not rule out such certificates geometrically or change the C-II frontier; canonical source applications require the independent hypotheses of `C-DISJOINT-SWAP-2SAT`. See the [independent Audit.99](../../audits/target_c_zero_valid_pinned_closure_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.100 promotes `C-GENERIC-ISOLATE-ANCHOR-AVOIDANCE`, `C-SHORT-WALL-WEAK-CLEARANCE-CLASSIFICATION`, `C-LOADED-UNIT-ANCHOR-STRICT-OBTUSE`, and `C-STATIC-SINGLETON-UNLOADED-ANCHOR`. Generic isolate motion excludes contact isolates as exact unit anchors in a locally pinned static singleton exchange. For `D>=10000`, three loaded unit anchors are strictly obtuse, so the pinned exchange also needs an unloaded core anchor. Inside an intact simple wall of at most six edges of length at most one, the only weak-clearance site is the regular unit hexagon center and insertion makes a unit triangle. No result supplies such a site, packet capacity, or a global competitor; the C-II frontier remains open. See the [independent Audit.100](../../audits/target_c_generic_isolate_anchor_avoidance_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.101 promotes `C-FAN-REFLECTION-STRICT-CAPACITY-LOSS-REGRESSION`. An explicit ten-point nonglobal family has an actual diameter-fan leaf and an eight-edge unit wall with a strictly safe isolate. The fan reflection retains the wall contacts and full-source diameter upper bound but collides with that isolate; six unit-circumradius triangles cover the reflected wall, leaving no strict safe interior site. This shows old wall clearance and upper nonexpansiveness do not transport wall capacity. The example has no positive KKT or canonical full-core optimality hypothesis and does not refute the canonical fan theorem or exclude unrestricted relocation. See the [independent Audit.101](../../audits/target_c_fan_reflection_capacity_loss_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.102 promotes `C-THIRD-CONTACT-FAN-REFLECTION-COLLISION-REGRESSION`. Whenever a unit-separated source of diameter `D>=2` contains a fan leaf `p` with two unit neighbors at diameter distance from a common carrier `a` and a third unit neighbor `z`, the third neighbor is strictly inside `conv{a,p_-,p_+}`. The fan reflection across the two designated neighbors keeps their contacts and all diameter upper bounds but moves `p` to within distance less than one of `z`. This is an exact finite-geometry guardrail for the degree-two fan scope, with no global-source or population claim. See the [independent Audit.102](../../audits/target_c_third_fan_contact_reflection_collision_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.103 promotes `C-LOADED-ANCHOR-SPARSE-PACKET-OBSTRUCTION`. For canonical `D>=9`, `C=C_*`, `H=H_*` C-II sources, if an equal-cardinality replacement deletes at least one contact, every inserted-involving pair is unit-separated and strictly below `D`, and every retained nonisolated core endpoint of a new contact is loaded, then its inserted-involving contact count satisfies `J(J+2L_D)>=D/4`, with `L_D=sqrt(D*eta_D+eta_D^2)` and `eta_D=3^(1/4)*sqrt(D)`. The all-loaded-core threshold is `J >= sqrt(L_D^2+D/4)-L_D = (1/(8*3^(1/8))+o(1))*D^(1/4)`. The proof retains/moves every source isolate and derives finite release from a `1/2`-wide strip of straight positive-stress chains. It neither produces a packet nor handles unloaded retained core anchors or inserted-involving diameter pairs; C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.103](../../audits/target_c_loaded_anchor_sparse_packet_obstruction_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.104 promotes two full-source diameter-endpoint incidence bounds. For each fixed `1<t<sqrt(2)` and all sufficiently large noncollinear global minimizers, any source point has at most two actual diameter endpoints within distance `t`; any two diameter endpoints within `t` are consecutive full-source hull vertices. The threshold depends only on `t`, and no source isolates are omitted. The shorter loaded-hull-side consequence is compatible with canonical `C-LOADED-NEAR-UNIT-ADJACENCY`, while full-source hull consecutivity is stronger. These are geometry inputs only; owner-capacity counting remains separate. See the [independent Audit.104](../../audits/target_c_loaded_endpoint_local_incidence_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.105 promotes `C-OO-OUTER-ISOLATE-COLLAR-BOUND` in the Type-II near-disk branch: with `x_B=b-q` and `a` nonunit loaded-hull sides, at most `48(x_B+a)` distinct contact isolates lie within distance `5/4` of the convex outer pockets and nondegenerate exterior caps. Under the canonical small O--O isolate assignment, every isolate lower-clause endpoint lies in these collars. Thus `x_B+a=o(D)` leaves only `o(D)` distinct isolate blockers. By itself this does not charge owners or prove an O--O proposal family. See the [independent Audit.105](../../audits/target_c_outer_isolate_collar_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

Audit.106 promotes `C-OO-LOWER-WITNESS-OWNER-CAPACITY` for an already specified disjoint-owner O--O family. If its singleton proposals preserve all contacts and upper bounds, pass unary rhombus tests, and strictly shorten old diameters, then lower-witness charging gives `k<=2m_B+3(x_B+Z_out)`. In the Type-II near-disk branch, Audit.105 confines all isolate witnesses to the defect-sized collar, improving the bound to `k<=147(x_B+m_B)`; thus `x_B+m_B=o(D)` rules out a linear counted family. Audit.114 promotes the source report's `2d_H` deletion corollary under fixed subfamily-stability and canonical isolate dispatch, giving `r<=2m_B+3(x_B+Z_out)+2d_H` (or `r<=147(x_B+m_B)+2d_H` in Type II). Neither theorem produces the proposal family or shows that the low-defect condition holds. C-II stays open. See [Audit.106](../../audits/target_c_outer_owner_witness_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md) and [Audit.114](../../audits/target_c_original_family_outer_owner_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-07.md).

## Recent reduced-source stress deletion

Audits.170–171 close the exceptional-face gap once a nonempty loaded set `W` is supplied. `C-ALMOST-ACCESSIBLE-STRESS-TRANSVERSAL` chooses at most `s_Z+1` deletions across `W` and the unloaded positive-contact core, with at most `min(b_0,s_Z)` unloaded exceptions; the reduced full source then has a strict first motion. It uses contact-row independence and the full actual diameter graph, retaining contact isolates. It proves neither source-level `W` extraction nor cardinality restoration. The stress-transversal application still needs stress-relevant access to `W` and compatible cardinality restoration. Audit.173 now proves unweighted approximate Type-II grid access for all but `eta D` actual diameter edges; the exceptional edges may carry all positive stress mass. See the [candidate report](../../reports/target_c_almost_accessible_transversal_face_codimension_2026-10-08.md) and [independent Audit.171](../../audits/target_c_almost_accessible_transversal_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.172 further promotes `C-DIAMETER-STAR-STRESS-CHAIN-CLASSIFICATION` and `C-STAR-EXCEPTIONAL-FACE-ONE-UNLOADED-TRANSVERSAL`: when `E_0=H[B\W]` is empty or lies in one actual diameter star, the deletion bound uses at most one unloaded core label. Two exact regressions keep the triangle-free and star-support hypotheses explicit. The claim does not extract such a star or restore deleted labels; C-II remains open. See the [promoted report](../../reports/target_c_exceptional_diameter_star_face_2026-10-08.md) and [independent Audit.172](../../audits/target_c_exceptional_diameter_star_face_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.173 promotes `C-UNIFORM-HARMONIC-CELL-AREA`, `C-TYPEII-LOCAL-COHERENT-GRID-ACCESS`, `C-TYPEII-ACTUAL-DIAMETER-GRID-ACCESS`, `C-TYPEII-FACE-TAIL-PORT-MULTIPLICITY`, and `C-TYPEII-ATTACHMENT-COHERENT-PROPER-CAP`: every nonempty unloaded attachment cell has fixed positive quadratic area in both C-II branches; in Type II, every attachment has a nearby same-component linear-by-linear coherent grid, all but `eta D` actual diameter edges have both endpoints routed to their grids by short contact paths, all but `eta D` attachment incidences have short local routes to every nearby component vertex, and every attachment has a proper loaded-chord cap containing quadratic same-component coherent mass at positive linear height. The audit repairs the source proof's unsupported crop of a selected grain hull by cropping the cell and using the global deficit and interface budgets. The endpoint edge exception is unweighted, and the face-tail result counts incidences rather than independent route currency. No stress-mass bound, finite move, refill, or C-II closure follows. The source's “many accessible grids” claim is subsumed by the promoted port and grid theorems. See the [candidate reports](../../reports/target_c_uniform_harmonic_cells_global_diameter_access_2026-10-08.md) and [face-tail port multiplicity report](../../reports/target_c_typeii_face_tail_port_multiplicity_2026-10-08.md), plus the [uniform-cell audit](../../audits/target_c_uniform_harmonic_cells_global_diameter_access_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md) and [face-tail/cap audit](../../audits/target_c_typeii_face_tail_port_multiplicity_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

