Audit.170 promotes `C-ACCESSIBLE-STRESS-FACE-DICHOTOMY` and records `C-SEVEN-SITE-TAGGED-STRESS-MASS-REGRESSION`. For any supplied set `W` of actual loaded endpoints, either an `s_Z+1`-site transversal can be selected inside `W`, leaving a strict first motion of the reduced full source, or a nonempty stress face forces every extreme circuit to have the explicit defect `sigma_* >= D*cos((b_0-1)*asin(1/(2D)))-(b_0+1)/2` when at most `D` active diameter edges avoid `W`. This does not extract `W` from grids or restore deleted labels. The exact seven-site example has triangles and no globality claim; it only blocks generic mass inheritance from positive support and PSD affine-kernel data. C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.170](../../audits/target_c_accessible_stress_face_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md) and [source report](../../reports/target_c_accessible_stress_transversal_or_degenerate_face_2026-10-08.md).

Audit.234 promotes the fixed-`M` central-vacancy sorting classification for the specified arithmetic six-sided wall. It assumes convergence to the punctured triangular chart and supplies no arbitrary one-deficit extraction, source move, or frontier change; see the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_sorted_rate_central_vacancy_independent_audit_2026-10-10.md).

## Binding rejected implications

Do not use without a new proof:

- incident-diameter sequence `=>` Euler trail/path;
- FB4 force-bulk localization;
- the incompatible-cut C53 pentagon objection;
- RSR disk-equality uniqueness;
- Eppstein near-equality `=>` near quadrangulation;
- five-host occupancy from angular-measure saturation;
- inward OP pocket `subset` exterior Reuleaux minor cap;
- deletion of contact-isolated source points;
- full-source extension of the §57 affine-kernel theorem;
- `r/(6C_T)` as the sharp limit of the Section-90 bin-counting method.
- reduced grain phase coherence, positive pinned equilibrium, and full pinned rank imply a single-lattice physical-position cover of the grain; the bent-grid regression refutes this reduced-input implication.
- the bent-grid regression neither refutes positional covers of canonical actual loaded endpoints nor rules out bounded multiple-lattice covers.

The PR #191 `0.995540...` theorem remains rejected. The finite `q=13` S1 computation remains a regression, not a global-minimizer theorem.

### Audit.174 — Type-II separate-face floor

`C-TYPEII-ATOMIC-SEPARATE-FACE-FLOOR` gives `liminf (f-k)/D>=6s_2/5` and `liminf (2(q-k)-k-z)/D>=pi+6s_2/5`. It follows that `(q-k)/D` has lower limit at least `pi/2+3s_2/5>2pi/3`, eliminating the positive one-cell collar range in the full-core Type-II near-disk branch. Higher-`(q-k)/D` Type-II sequences and the macroscopic-container alternative remain open; the theorem gives no face-width, area, or source-move conclusion. See the [independent Audit.174](../../audits/target_c_typeii_terminal_cancellation_face_floor_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

### Audit.179 — cut-to-hull train banks

`C-TRAIN-CUT-HULL-BANK` gives a one-third selected-train bank whose terminals reach actual loaded vertices through a common physical contact carrier. `C-QUADRATIC-CORE-TRAIN-BANK` gives a linear bank of long trains in an `o(D^2)`-edge carrier whenever the strict-rhombus population is quadratic. The carrier can reuse bottlenecks; there are no disjoint-return or individual route-length bounds, and the finite-move/payment obligation remains open. See the [independent Audit.179](../../audits/target_c_train_cut_hull_bank_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).

### Audit.180 — long-train separators and disjoint returns

Audit.235 sharpens the Audit.180 bounds with the finite Oler inequality. `C-CORE-UNLOADED-SEPARATOR-AREA-BOUND` now gives `|U|<=b_*^2/(4*pi*a0)+b_*/2+c_K-k`, and hence `|U|<=8k^2/(pi*sqrt(3))+2k`, for every endpoint-inclusive contact cut of size `k`; here `a0=sqrt(3)/2` and Audit.235 defines the componentwise excess `b_*`. `C-LONG-TRAIN-SEPARATOR-INEQUALITY` becomes `(a-5k)_+(L+1)<=40k^2/(pi*sqrt(3))+10k` for `a` distinct trains of degree at least `L`. Combined with the Audit.179 bank, positive quadratic strict-rhombus bulk still gives linearly many vertex-disjoint actual-contact paths from long bank terminals to distinct loaded vertices, now with the explicit lower constant in `C-QUADRATIC-CORE-DISJOINT-TRAIN-RETURNS`. The paths are endpoint-inclusive in the full contact graph and need not lie in the Audit.179 carrier; a path can have length zero when its selected terminal is already loaded. They have no positive individual length or interface-payment bound and yield no source motion. The C-II finite-move/payment obligation remains open. See the [independent Audit.235](../../audits/target_c_oler_sharp_separator_independent_audit_2026-10-10.md) and the [Audit.180 proof](../../audits/target_c_long_train_separator_and_disjoint_returns_independent_audit_2026-10-08.md).


### Audit.186 — shared-carrier disjoint-return cost regression

`C-SHARED-CARRIER-DISJOINT-RETURN-COST-REGRESSION`: under artificial pins, a train-rich rational source with positive pinned equilibrium and rigidity has an `8m`-edge carrier, but its maximum family of full-contact disjoint returns costs `Omega(m^2)` edge occurrences. The family is nonglobal and its pins are not actual diameter endpoints. This rules out the reduced one-time carrier-edge payment inference under those weaker hypotheses only; it does not contradict the canonical train-bank or actual-loaded return theorems. The C-II finite-move/payment obligation is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.186](../../audits/target_c_shared_carrier_disjoint_return_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.187 — one-pin oblique full-interior refill

C-OBLIQUE-ONE-PIN-STRICT-REFILL gives an explicit two-stage motion that strictly opens every pair involving a movable interior label in an oblique rectangular unit grid after deleting one boundary pin. The crossing bound C-UNIT-SIDE-CROSSING-SEPARATION then gives C-ONE-PIN-OBLIQUE-WALL-POPULATION: every triangle-free contact-minimal global minimizer with a qualifying actual 2(m+n)-vertex cycle has at least (m-1)(n-1) points in its full interior population, including isolates. C-ONE-PIN-OBLIQUE-WALL-OLER-RESIDUAL gives the convex-wall bound using angular area excess and explicit vertex perturbation error. The separate C-TRIANGULAR-ONE-PIN-DISPLACEMENT-GAP-REGRESSION shows that no nonconstant continuous feasible motion exists in the triangular pinned chart. These exact wall results provide no extraction from arbitrary canonical sources, frame-overlap payment, or C-II closure. See the [independent Audit.187](../../audits/target_c_one_boundary_pin_oblique_refill_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.188 — moved-core exterior-cap refill regression

`C-EXTERIOR-CAP-MOVED-CORE-REFILL-NONGLOBAL-REGRESSION` gives, for every `M>=20`, a same-cardinality strict-sub-`2M` endpoint after deleting two seam guards and translating the retained core shores, while retaining every scaled isolate. A common small finite downward move of the two inserted sites opens their four contacts and leaves a triangle-free endpoint. This is the explicit six-spoke source already covered by the paired-cap regression; it fails canonical mixed rigidity and linear loaded-diameter density, so it supplies no canonical refill or cap extraction. The source proof's claimed opposite-shore normal separation `>3` is stronger than needed and is corrected in the independent audit to a lower bound `14/5>1`. See the [independent Audit.188](../../audits/target_c_exterior_cap_moved_core_refill_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.191 — simultaneous upper release for small packets

`C-SMALL-PACKET-SIMULTANEOUS-UPPER-RELEASE` gives a common finite inward/dilation move for packets with strict metric separation from retained points and each other, provided the containing radius plus twice the packet radius is below `D`. `C-SMALL-PACKET-DIAMETER-COVER-OBSTRUCTION` then excludes any equal-cardinality replacement with packet radii at most `D/8` that covers all old actual diameter pairs, for every global minimizer with `D>=64`; triangle-freeness and contact-minimality are not needed. The contact-loss corollary is already subsumed by the canonical contact-budget endpoint. No packet existence or source capacity follows; C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.191](../../audits/target_c_small_packet_upper_release_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.192 — triangular two-boundary-hole refill

`C-TRIANGULAR-TWO-BOUNDARY-HOLE-STRICT-REFILL` releases every interior label of a fixed triangular `m`-by-`n` frame after deleting two adjacent side labels. For a nearby actual simple unit-contact wall in a triangle-free contact-minimal global minimizer, `C-TRIANGULAR-TWO-BOUNDARY-HOLE-POPULATION` forces at least `(m-1)(n-1)-1` interior source points, including isolates. The proof retains every exterior point and uses the canonical crossing-separation bound. The tolerance depends on the fixed frame; no wall extraction, growing-frame uniformity, or C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.192](../../audits/target_c_two_boundary_hole_triangular_refill_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.193 — two-hole strict-capacity regression

For arbitrary continuous targets strictly inside the triangular parallelogram and strictly separated from all retained boundary pins, the capacity after deleting adjacent pins is exactly `K=(m-1)(n-1)` for `m=3,n>=3` and exactly `9` for `4`-by-`4`. A complete triangulation assigns every admissible target to a unit triangle; in the `4`-by-`4` frame, six of ten admissible triangles form a unit-disk star that cannot hold six strictly separated points. This prevents a universal `K+1` fixed-frame site-count argument in the stated cases, but supplies no global-minimizer counterexample and no obstruction when both frame dimensions grow. See the [independent Audit.193](../../audits/target_c_two_boundary_hole_strict_capacity_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.194 — polynomial-margin two-side oblique refill

`C-OBLIQUE-TWO-SIDE-POLYNOMIAL-STRICT-REFILL` simultaneously strictifies the full interior of an oblique rectangular grid after deleting its lower/left boundary bank. Its squared margin is `3/(250000*L^14)` for every `pi/3<=phi<=pi/2`, including the triangular endpoint, and its actual-wall tolerance is explicit and uniform in the angle. A qualifying nearby actual simple unit-contact cycle in a triangle-free contact-minimal global minimizer forces `z>=mn-2m-2n+3`, counting all interior source points. The convex-wall corollary gives the exact residual upper bound with an `L-2` boundary term. At `phi=pi/3`, the population bound is weaker than Audit.192's fixed-frame `z>=K-1`; Audit.194 adds a growing-frame tolerance, not a stronger fixed-frame capacity result. No wall extraction or C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.194](../../audits/target_c_two_side_polynomial_refill_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.195 — one-vacancy oblique strictification and capacity gap

`C-OBLIQUE-ONE-VACANCY-STRICTIFICATION` releases every interior contact of the complete oblique rectangular grid except its already absent interior label, by first moving the vacancy row and column and then expanding the remaining blocks in the dual basis. It fixes the entire boundary and is valid for `pi/3<phi<2*pi/3`. Using the canonical near-triangular full-wall lock regression and Oler's bound, `C-OBLIQUE-ONE-VACANCY-STRICT-CAPACITY-GAP` gives weak fixed-wall capacity `(m-1)(n-1)` and strict capacity exactly one smaller, for each fixed frame in a positive non-effective angle window. Square frames have quadratic strict interior population behind a linear wall, but the wall is not certified as actual diameter support and the configurations are not global minimizers. This does not change the source-level C-II endpoint. See the [independent Audit.195](../../audits/target_c_one_vacancy_oblique_grid_strictification_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.196 — strict wall packet activation

`C-UNIT-WALL-PACKET-POPULATION-BOUND` gives a source-level population rule for any simple actual unit-contact wall: if a strict interior packet has `M` sites, the source wall interior has at least `M` points, and equality leaves only contact isolates. For an exact oblique rectangular grid wall, `C-EXACT-OBLIQUE-WALL-POPULATION` and one-vacancy strictification force the full count `(m-1)(n-1)` for `m,n>=2` and `pi/3<phi<2*pi/3`. `C-TRIANGULAR-TWO-INTERIOR-HOLE-STRICTIFICATION` adds a finite fixed-container release at the triangular angle with two prescribed interior holes. The crossing lemma in the source report was already canonical, its weaker near-wall count is superseded by Audit.192, and its Oler uniqueness claim remains conditional. These results do not extract a complete wall from arbitrary sources or change the C-II payment obligation. See the [independent Audit.196](../../audits/target_c_funded_unit_wall_activation_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.198 — finite train-cut traffic compression

`C-TRAIN-CUT-TRAFFIC-COMPRESSION` gives a positive semidefinite compression and exact kernel for the canonical core stress on an explicitly admissible disjoint tree-cut family. The resulting finite contact-preserving translations decrease the normalized weighted mean of the old diameter-pair squares; this does not control the maximum or provide an upper-feasible endpoint. No cut-family extraction or C-II frontier change follows. See the [independent audit](../../audits/target_c_train_cut_traffic_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.199 — two-thirds global population reserve

`C-GLOBAL-POPULATION-EXCESS-TWO-THIRDS-RESERVE` proves that every global unit-separated minimizer with `D>=2` has `g_A>=kappa*D^(2/3)` for an absolute positive constant. The proof builds an independent strict-sub-`D` translated-lattice subset and then applies the all-minimizer quantifier. Audit.245 registers that competitor construction separately as `C-TRIANGULAR-LATTICE-STRICT-D-COMPETITOR-RESERVE` and records its triangle-free deformation as a packing-class regression. Audit.199 itself still does not modify a source, imply a unit triangle, or change the C-II payment obligation. The older explicit square-root reserve remains canonical because its stated finite-range constant is not uniformly compared to the new unspecified constant. See the [independent Audit.199](../../audits/target_c_population_excess_two_thirds_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.200 — square-root harmonic-component count

`C-HARMONIC-COMPONENT-SQRT-COUNT` improves the post-ZF unloaded-component count to `c_I=O(sqrt(D))` by using the pairwise interior-disjoint actual attachment cells and a curvature budget on each force-hull arc. It improves the component term in the pinned ledger but does not bound contact isolates or component populations. No source move, C-II payment, or Level I consequence follows. See the [independent Audit.200](../../audits/target_c_harmonic_component_sqrt_count_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.201 — Type-II single-cell inverse-phase exclusion

`C-SLOW-LATTICE-DIAMETER-ENDPOINT-BOUND` says that a fixed number of unit-rhombic lattice charts with `K/D`-Lipschitz displacement can contain at most `O(D^(4/5))` actual diameter endpoints, apart from uncovered endpoints; no chart cover is extracted. `C-TYPEII-SINGLE-CELL-INVERSE-PHASE-EXCLUSION` rules out a unit triangular six-direction frame within `K/D` of every actual internal and attachment contact of one whole unloaded harmonic cell, for any fixed `K` along an unbounded canonical Type-II sequence. The latter proof uses the uniform linear attachment lower bound, the exceptional-incidence local-route theorem, and a local curved-boundary lattice count; it requires no global face-tail hypothesis or zero holonomy. Selected grids and contacts in other cells are unrestricted. Neither result gives a source motion or C-II closure. See the [independent Audit.201](../../audits/target_c_typeii_single_cell_inverse_phase_exclusion_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.202 — global inverse-phase and face-tail exclusion

`C-GLOBAL-INVERSE-PHASE-FACE-TAIL-EXCLUSION` rules out the joint conditional package of one `K/D`-accurate frame for every actual core contact and a vanishing tail of the actual hull-augmented face-path lengths. The face tail gives zero lattice holonomy; an actual-contact routing bound then yields one globally injective `O(1/D)`-Lipschitz lattice chart for all loaded endpoints. Audit.201's curved-boundary count makes `q=o(D)`, contradicting the canonical diameter-edge lower bound. Neither global premise is currently extracted, so C-II and Level I remain open. Contact isolates stay in the source and in the ball-hull geometry. See the [independent Audit.202](../../audits/target_c_global_inverse_phase_face_tail_exclusion_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.203 — actual loaded boundary-walk directional strain

`C-LOADED-WALK-DIRECTIONAL-STRAIN` proves that any closed actual unit-contact walk visiting at least `gamma D` actual diameter endpoints in convex cyclic order has occurrence-counted strain at least `kappa(gamma)D` from every unit-rhombic lattice, uniformly over its orientation and even when the lattice varies with `D`. `C-CANONICAL-LOADED-WALK-STRAIN` applies this to the full positive-contact core boundary and to each Type-II harmonic cell using only internal and attachment contacts. Each application yields linearly many distinct actual contacts a fixed positive distance from the lattice direction set. The proof uses full-source supporting normals and midpoint localization, retains contact isolates, and charges the cyclic closing chord without assuming zero holonomy. It constructs no move, compatible packet, or payment and does not change the C-II endpoint. See the [independent Audit.203](../../audits/target_c_loaded_boundary_walk_directional_strain_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.204 — all-branch hull-train depletion

`C-ALL-BRANCH-HULL-TRAIN-DEPLETION` shows that, in either exhaustive post-ZF C-II branch, only `o(D^2)` rhombi cross trains carrying an actual unit loaded-hull side. Removing those trains only from the auxiliary simple triangle-free crossing graph leaves `N_0>=2sqrt(R_4)-o(D)`. The exact ledgers give `5(q-k)+x_B+m_H+2>=3P_F+2sqrt(R_4)-o(D)` and `4mu_DD>=2P_F+2sqrt(R_4)+k+3m_H-o(D)`. This extends hull-train depletion beyond the Type-II near-disk scope; it constructs no source capacity or motion, so C-II remains open. Audit.204 also corrects Audit.202's endpoint dependency: strict full-source support and `C-RHOMBUS-POP` imply every actual diameter endpoint belongs to the positive-contact core; no generic isolate perturbation input is required. See the [independent Audit.204](../../audits/target_c_all_branch_hull_train_depletion_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.205 — actual normals and all-branch separate-face floor

`C-CII-ACTUAL-DIAMETER-NORMAL-ATOMLESS` gives atomless weak limits for actual diameter normals in either C-II branch, without a loaded-hull disk premise. `C-ALL-BRANCH-SEPARATE-FACE-FLOOR` then proves `f-k>=6/5*sqrt(R_4)-o(D)`, `2(q-k)-k-z>=P_F+6/5*sqrt(R_4)-o(D)`, and `q-k>=P_F/2+3/5*sqrt(R_4)+(k+z)/2-o(D)`. It retains interior-only face components and all source points. Audit.174 is the Type-II specialization; Type I keeps its actual perimeter and rhombus population. These asymptotic bounds give no face width, vacancy, capacity, or finite source move, so C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.205](../../audits/target_c_actual_normal_separate_face_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.206 — sharper full-source midpoint localization and quantitative train depletion

`C-FS-DIAMETER-MIDPOINT-D2-3` sharpens the universal full-source diameter-midpoint radius to `7D^(2/3)` for every noncollinear global minimizer with `D>=7`, and gives `Delta(H)<=56D^(2/3)+1` for `D>=21952`. The same radius sharpens the all-branch hull-train estimate to `R_H=O(D^(5/3))` and `N_0>=2sqrt(R_4)-O(D^(5/6))`; the two exact ledger inequalities inherit that error. Contact-isolated points stay in the full-source geometry, while graph deletion remains auxiliary. The stronger rate gives no capacity, motion, or strict-D competitor, so C-II remains open. Section 11.3's matching and odd-girth bounds are weaker than canonical results and are not separately promoted. See the [independent Audit.206](../../audits/target_c_midpoint_and_quantitative_hull_train_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.207 — all-branch hull-face terminal and pentagon budget

The short-unit-polygon direction lemma bounds near-parallel edges by two for pentagons and hexagons and by `d-4` for every degree `d>=7`. Full-source radialization aligns each bounded terminal face normal with the corresponding unit loaded-hull side. Combining those inputs with Audit.206's `R_H=O(D^(5/3))` gives `E_HF<=Q+f_5+O(D^(5/6))` and the exact end-count consequences `f_5>=2(k-m)-O(D^(5/6))`, `q+m>=4k+u_B-O(D^(5/6))`. This is asymptotic and retains the five-train pentagon regression by explicitly charging long trains and large faces; it yields no source motion or C-II closure. See the [independent Audit.207](../../audits/target_c_hull_face_end_pentagon_budget_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.208 — single-port lattice chain obstruction

`C-UNIT-CHAIN-TRIANGULAR-LATTICE-DIRECTION` proves that five lattice ports outside a seven-site unit chain, with each consecutive anchor pair distinct, force the chain direction to be one of the triangular-lattice unit directions. Its full-source compactness consequence aligns every side in a qualifying actual loaded seven-vertex window under a fixed finite orientation atlas. The resulting `C-SINGLE-PORT-ATLAS-OBSTRUCTION` gives `q<=6m+5b_0+4R_D+o(D)` when the local approximation holds uniformly. It does not extract that atlas or any sublinear defects, so no source motion or C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.208](../../audits/target_c_single_port_lattice_chain_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.209 — three-port finite direction bound

For three distinct fixed anchors and contact indices `0<r<s`, `C-THREE-PORT-FINITE-DIRECTION-BOUND` limits the feasible unit chain directions to at most ten. The proof includes the determinant-singular directions and excludes the rational identity case. The repeated-anchor variant is not included, and the finite bound extracts no actual source chain or motion. See the [independent Audit.209](../../audits/target_c_three_port_finite_direction_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.210 — finite-atlas single-port scarcity

`C-FINITE-ATLAS-SINGLE-PORT-SCARCITY` shows that a fixed finite rhombic atlas with uniform local approximation on every bounded loaded unit run cannot contain a linear number of chosen actual single ports if nonunit loaded-hull sides are `o(D)`. It handles arbitrary chart changes and does not require consecutive ports. The corrected finite block ledger charges `2m` additional endpoints at nonunit block boundaries. The atlas and side-saturation premise remain unextracted; no source motion or C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.210](../../audits/target_c_finite_atlas_single_port_scarcity_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.211 — repeated anchors and near-unit port scarcity

`C-THREE-PORT-REPEATED-ANCHOR-DIRECTION-BOUND` allows repeated anchors when the first anchor is also separated by at least one from the middle chain site; under that explicit condition, at most ten directions occur. `C-NEAR-UNIT-ATLAS-PORT-SCARCITY` shows that, under a fixed finite rhombic atlas with uniform translated-lattice approximation on every bounded loaded window, `P_F-q=o(D)` forces the chosen actual single-port count to be `o(D)`. The consequence from linear port count is only a positive liminf for `(P_F-q)/D`, with no explicit constant. The atlas and saturation premise remain unextracted; no source move or C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.211](../../audits/target_c_near_unit_atlas_port_scarcity_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.212 — bounded-variation chart port scarcity

`C-ACTUAL-DIAMETER-ENDPOINT-STRIP-BOUND` bounds the actual diameter endpoints in any strip of width `w` by `4*sqrt(2Dw)+4w+4`. `C-BOUNDED-VARIATION-CHART-PORT-SCARCITY` uses that estimate to remove ports near the finitely many large shore increments, then applies Audit.211 to the remaining locally translated charts. Under a fixed chart count, uniform total-variation bound, and `P_F-q=o(D)`, only `o(D)` loaded vertices have a chosen charted actual unloaded unit neighbor. No chart or saturation premise is extracted, and there is no source motion or C-II closure. See the [independent Audit.212](../../audits/target_c_bounded_variation_chart_port_scarcity_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.213 — tau-D binary loaded-owner side pocket budget

`C-TAU-D-BINARY-OWNER-SIDE-POCKET-BUDGET` counts every distinct loaded endpoint pair with distinct nonzero owners and `1<|a-b|<tau_D`, where `tau_D=3D/(sqrt(3D^2+4)+1)`. The canonical loaded-near-unit theorem makes each pair a loaded-hull side; the acute binary window excludes a length-two outer interval because its two unit contact rays have projective separation greater than `pi/3`. Distinct physical pairs then each spend one unit of `u_B`. This subsumes the old `13/12` lower-witness theorem. Unary clauses and general crossing multishears remain outside, and no finite source move follows. See the [independent Audit.213](../../audits/target_c_tau_d_binary_loaded_witness_pocket_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.214 — bounded-variation lattice endpoint count

`C-CURVED-BOUNDARY-LATTICE-LOCAL-CAPACITY` bounds actual diameter endpoints in one local curved-boundary patch when they lie near one translated rhombic lattice. `C-BOUNDED-VARIATION-LATTICE-ENDPOINT-COUNT` applies it to an actual additive chart with fixed total shore variation and gives `O_M(D^(4/5))` occupied endpoints. The full source is retained, but no chart extraction, port multiplicity, source move, or C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.214](../../audits/target_c_bounded_variation_lattice_endpoint_count_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.215 — angular surplus and bounded chart variation

`C-ANGULAR-SURPLUS-BOUNDED-SHORE-VARIATION` turns a linear actual-rhombus surplus budget and linear incidence at every chart increment into a uniform total shore-variation bound. Audit.214's endpoint count then excludes a Type-II harmonic cell whose loaded attachments occupy one actual chart under the stated phase and face-incidence assumptions. Neither the actual chart nor the face-incidence package is extracted from a canonical cell; C-II remains open. See the [independent Audit.215](../../audits/target_c_angular_surplus_bounded_variation_chart_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.216 — bounded-variation contact-port counts

C-NEAR-BOUNDARY-LATTICE-LOCAL-CAPACITY extends the local lattice count to unit-separated points near the boundary of a macroscopic disk intersection. C-POSITIVE-LEAF-ANCHOR-INNER-BALL-BOUNDARY places the sole positive-stress contact anchor of a loaded leaf within O(D^(-1/2)) of the full-source inner ball hull. Together they give O_{J,M}(D^(4/5)) leaf ports in a fixed finite bounded-variation chart family. The separate double-port estimate is O_{J,M}(D^(6/7)) for arbitrary unit-separated sources and does not assume a midpoint guard. All source points remain retained. The charts are not extracted, and no source move or C-II closure follows. See the [independent Audit.216](../../audits/target_c_bounded_variation_contact_port_counts_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.217 — occupied midpoint guards

`C-FULLY-OCCUPIED-CHART-DOUBLE-PORT-COUNT` improves the arbitrary-source double-port count to `O_{J,M}(D^(4/5))` when every position in each bounded-variation rectangle is occupied by a distinct actual source point. The rounded coefficient midpoint is then an actual guard, keeping a remaining endpoint uniformly away from circle tangency. Together with the canonical leaf-port count and `q>=h>=ceil(D)`, this gives `C-FULLY-OCCUPIED-CHART-CONTACT-PORT-SCARCITY`: at least `D-O_{J,M}(D^(4/5))` loaded endpoints are uncovered by the stated leaf-or-two-chart-neighbors alternative. Full chart occupancy is assumed and is not extracted; no source move, C-II closure, or Level-I result follows. See the [independent Audit.217](../../audits/target_c_occupied_midpoint_guard_double_port_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.218 — slow-chart double-port count

`C-SLOW-CHART-DOUBLE-PORT-COUNT` bounds actual diameter endpoints with two unit neighbors in one of a fixed number of arbitrary-domain, pointwise `K/D`-Lipschitz rhombic charts by `O_{J,K}(D^(4/5))`. It preserves the square-root error at circle tangency and does not require a complete rectangle. On canonical C-II sources, `C-CII-SLOW-CHART-DOUBLE-PORT-COVER-EXCLUSION` therefore leaves at least `D-O_{J,K}(D^(4/5))` loaded endpoints outside this two-port coverage class. The chart family is assumed, not extracted; C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.218](../../audits/target_c_slow_chart_double_port_count_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.219 — near-triangular double-port localization

`C-NEAR-TRIANGULAR-CHART-DOUBLE-PORT-LOCALIZATION` places any source point with two distinct unit neighbors in a supplied `K/D`-slow chart whose basis angle is within `K_0/D` of `pi/3` at distance `O_{K,K_0}(D^(-1/2))` from a full-lattice point, with the neighbor's chart displacement frozen at its anchor. This is a local positional statement; it does not extract charts or yield a source move. The report's endpoint-count corollary is subsumed by Audit.218's broader rhombic count. The C-II frontier is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.219](../../audits/target_c_near_triangular_chart_double_port_localization_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.220 — slow-chart near-boundary count

C-SLOW-CHART-NEAR-BOUNDARY-COUNT bounds any unit-separated set within distance rho<=1 of a well-rounded O(D) disk-intersection boundary, when at most J supplied K/D-Lipschitz rhombic charts approximate each point within tau<=1/16. Its bound is O(D^(4/5)+D*rho+D*tau), and it depends on the canonical near-boundary lattice capacity theorem. It assumes the chart cover and supplies no extraction, source move, or strict diameter decrease; the C-II frontier is unchanged. See the [independent Audit.220](../../audits/target_c_slow_chart_near_boundary_count_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.221 — actual additive grid to slow chart (duplicate)

The report's Lemma 6.1 at frozen source commit 6132201c1d3056ca68a222217094acb819f5b4d7 is **PASS — already subsumed** by canonical C-ADDITIVE-CHART-RELATIVE-CHORD-BOUND (Audit.190); no new theorem ID is needed. For reference basis matrix T=[u v], label difference z, and physical chord r, Audit.190 gives |r-Tz|<=sqrt(2)*eta*|z|. The rhombic Gram bound gives |Tz|>=|z|/sqrt(2), so |r-Tz|<=2*eta*|Tz|. Setting eta=K0/D recovers Lemma 6.1 with K=2K0 once its fixed small-error threshold holds; the arbitrary-subset rectangular scope in Audit.190 includes this grid. The deduction is exact algebra and needs no separate verifier. It supplies no chart extraction or source move. See the [frozen report](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/blob/6132201c1d3056ca68a222217094acb819f5b4d7/docs/reports/target_c_localized_curved_boundary_slow_lattice_deformation_2026-10-09.md#6-actual-additive-inverse-phase-grids-satisfy-the-deformation-hypothesis) and the [canonical Audit.190](../../audits/target_c_relative_chord_diameter_direction_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.190 — relative-chord diameter directions

`C-ADDITIVE-CHART-RELATIVE-CHORD-BOUND` controls short physical chord directions from local increment error without requiring small accumulated position error. `C-RELATIVE-CHORD-DIAMETER-COUNT` applies the normal-cone count to the full actual diameter graph of canonical C-II sources, and `C-RELATIVE-CHORD-GROWING-CHART-EXCLUSION` rules out chart covers with `o(D)` exceptional endpoints, `o(D)` charts, total carrier perimeter `O(D)`, and total angular width `o(1)`. The candidate's broader arbitrary-source count is narrowed because its diameter-direction and hereditary edge bounds are canonical C-II inputs. No such charts have been extracted from arbitrary sources; C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.190](../../audits/target_c_relative_chord_diameter_direction_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).

### Audit.189 — quadratic-core cap extraction and deep returns

`C-QUADRATIC-CORE-LOADED-CHORD-CAP-EXTRACTION` selects a proper loaded-hull chord cap from the same harmonic attachment cell that contains the canonical quadratic coherent grain. `C-QUADRATIC-CORE-DEEP-CAP-GRID-RETURNS` crops that cap and yields a linear-by-linear physical grid, linearly many vertex-disjoint actual-contact paths to distinct loaded endpoints, and quadratic source population outside the entire cropped region. `C-QUADRATIC-CORE-CELL-BOUNDARY-STRIP-POPULATION` gives a separate quadratic population bound near the boundary of the whole harmonic cell. The source Type-II uniform near-port grid claim is already covered by Audit.173. These population statements supply no packet capacity, payment, source move, or strict-D competitor; C-II and Level I remain open. See the [independent Audit.189](../../audits/target_c_quadratic_core_cap_extraction_independent_audit_2026-10-09.md).


### Audit.243 — explicit asymptotic coefficient for the population reserve

`C-GLOBAL-POPULATION-EXCESS-ASYMPTOTIC-COEFFICIENT` proves that every sufficiently large global unit-separated diameter minimizer satisfies `g_A >= kappa0*D^(2/3)-C_theta*D^theta` for any fixed `131/208<theta<2/3`, where `kappa0=(3*pi/(5*a0))*(3*a0/16)^(2/3)` and `a0=sqrt(3)/2`. The proof uses Huxley's translated-lattice disk discrepancy only for a fixed centered triangular-lattice disk, then weights the thin conflict annulus by the exact two-disk lens area. This sharpens the asymptotic coefficient of Audit.199 but supplies no source move, triangle conclusion, or C-II payment. The separate one-lattice shell claims in its source report remain unreviewed. See the [independent Audit.243](../../audits/target_c_population_excess_sharp_coefficient_independent_audit_2026-10-10.md).

### Audit.244 — unit-edge hulls with a single lattice interior

`C-UNIT-EDGE-HULL-SINGLE-LATTICE-INTERIOR-RADIAL-EXCESS` proves that a global unit-separated minimizer whose convex hull has only unit edges, and whose non-hull points lie in one triangular lattice of spacing at least one, must have `R^2-D^2/4 >= c*D^(2/3)` for every containing radius-`R` disk and sufficiently large `D`. This excludes regular odd unit-side shells with that single-lattice interior, but establishes no lattice structure for a general source and no C-II payment. Contact-isolated points are included in the count. See the [independent Audit.244](../../audits/target_c_unit_edge_lattice_interior_shell_exclusion_independent_audit_2026-10-10.md).


### Audit.245 — triangle-free lattice competitor reserve

`C-TRIANGULAR-LATTICE-STRICT-D-COMPETITOR-RESERVE` supplies a finite unit-separated strict-sub-`D` competitor with at least `pi*D^2/(2*sqrt(3))+kappa*D^(2/3)` sites for every `D>=2`. Deforming its finite triangular-lattice basis slightly makes the unit-contact graph bipartite without changing cardinality or losing the strict diameter margin. This records `C-TRIANGLE-FREE-PACKING-DENSITY-CAP-REGRESSION`: triangle-free packings alone cannot obey a uniform density ceiling with `o(D^(2/3))` excess. The packing is not claimed to be globally optimal, and this does not bear on source-level C-II closure. See the [independent Audit.245](../../audits/target_c_triangle_free_lattice_competitor_reserve_independent_audit_2026-10-10.md).
